WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.940
- Hello everyone. Thanks for joining us.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.770
My name is Kaveh Khoshnood.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:14.464
I'm a faculty at the Yale
School of Public Health.

00:00:14.464 --> 00:00:18.180
I'm the faculty director for
Humanitarian Research Lab

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.250
at our School of Public Health,

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.789
and I've had the pleasure of
knowing Professor Claire Greene

00:00:23.789 --> 00:00:26.790
long time ago when she was a student,

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.663
an MPH student at our
School of Public Health.

00:00:29.663 --> 00:00:32.742
Professor Claire Greene
is an assistant professor

00:00:32.742 --> 00:00:36.390
in the program on forced
migration and health

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:40.260
at Columbia University Mailman
School of Public Health.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.838
She received her PhD in psychiatric

00:00:42.838 --> 00:00:45.090
and substance use epidemiology from

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:48.177
Johns Hopkins Bloomberg
School of Public Health.

00:00:48.177 --> 00:00:49.200
And as I said,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.560
she got her MPH from our
School of Public Health

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.250
in the Chronic Disease EPI
and Global Health Department,

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.590
and she did her postdoctoral training in

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.351
implementation science and

00:01:00.351 --> 00:01:02.973
humanitarian health from Columbia.

00:01:03.857 --> 00:01:07.740
At Columbia she teaches
courses under collection,

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:11.460
analysis and use of data to
improve humanitarian assistance

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.880
and on mental health and forced migration.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.840
Her research focuses on designing
and evaluating strategies

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.300
for improving the effectiveness
and implementation

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.817
of mental health and
substance use intervention

00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:25.738
in humanitarian settings.

00:01:25.738 --> 00:01:27.990
In addition to her research and teaching,

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.260
she regularly consults for human agencies

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.820
and humanitarian NGOs on
the design and evaluation

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:39.150
of mental health and
substance abuse program.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:41.100
Thank you so much, Claire, for joining us.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.250
I'll pass it on to you.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.300
- Okay, thank you.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:48.300
Thanks everyone who's here
and thanks to those online.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.600
It's truly a pleasure and
honor to be here today.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.720
So as Kaveh mentioned, it feels
very good to be back here.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.720
So I did my MPH here at Yale
and I have so many wonderful

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.339
memories of my time here,
one of them being a photo,

00:02:03.339 --> 00:02:05.860
it's at the top left of your screen.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.600
I did my thesis research
under the supervision of

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:12.000
Professor Trace Kirsha and
through the Downs Fellowship,

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.750
which was led at the time by Kaveh.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.560
In China, focused on mental health

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.870
and substance use and HIV.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.280
And the photo just below the,

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.440
on the bottom left-hand side of the screen

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.446
is actually my MPH thesis
poster presentation,

00:02:25.446 --> 00:02:28.043
which my parents were so proud of that

00:02:28.043 --> 00:02:30.347
they actually framed it

00:02:30.347 --> 00:02:32.970
and it's been hanging in their basement

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:34.080
for over 10 years now.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.690
So I guess I owe a big thank you to Yale

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.952
for helping me make my parents proud.

00:02:38.952 --> 00:02:40.755
But in all seriousness,

00:02:40.755 --> 00:02:44.370
it's really a pleasure to be
back here. Such fond memories.

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:47.040
And really, Yale was where I discovered

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.630
the fields of humanitarian health

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.370
and mental health and their intersection.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.340
I worked with the postdoc
at the McMillan Center

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.639
on a review trying to understand

00:02:55.639 --> 00:02:57.927
the alignment of mental
health interventions

00:02:57.927 --> 00:02:59.850
that are implemented in practice

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:01.270
and those that are researched

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:03.120
and perhaps unsurprisingly,

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.823
the evidence we have
does not align very well

00:03:04.823 --> 00:03:07.818
or didn't align very well
with those interventions

00:03:07.818 --> 00:03:10.871
that are actually implemented in practice.

00:03:10.871 --> 00:03:11.704
And for me,

00:03:11.704 --> 00:03:13.925
this is really what sparked my
interest and motivated me to

00:03:13.925 --> 00:03:16.315
move along this trajectory
in mental health

00:03:16.315 --> 00:03:18.270
and humanitarian settings,

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:21.570
which is one that I'm still
motivated and excited by today.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:24.003
So a big thanks to Yale and to all of you.

00:03:29.151 --> 00:03:30.780
All right, so I thought I'd start

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.570
just by talking about
why we should care about

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.966
mental health in the context
of humanitarian settings.

00:03:35.966 --> 00:03:38.490
Kaveh and I were just talking
about how there's such a

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:41.411
plethora of health needs in
these contexts, but for me,

00:03:41.411 --> 00:03:44.730
I think there are a few reasons
why mental health should

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:48.418
definitely be an essential part
of humanitarian assistance.

00:03:48.418 --> 00:03:50.513
The first is just
thinking about the burden.

00:03:50.513 --> 00:03:52.928
So the best estimates we have suggest that

00:03:52.928 --> 00:03:56.034
22% of people in conflict
affected settings

00:03:56.034 --> 00:04:00.018
have a mental disorder
that's more than three times

00:04:00.018 --> 00:04:02.274
higher than the general population.

00:04:02.274 --> 00:04:05.349
We know that even beyond that,

00:04:05.349 --> 00:04:09.480
people in humanitarian settings
experience elevated distress

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.640
and that affects their
functioning and their wellbeing.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:14.280
So there's a lot of people
affected and who could benefit

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.667
from mental health and
psychosocial supports.

00:04:16.667 --> 00:04:19.890
We also know that people in
humanitarian contexts have very

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.420
limited access to services.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:23.430
There are not a lot of specialists,

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:25.014
not a lot of mental health providers,

00:04:25.014 --> 00:04:28.060
fewer than 10% of people in
humanitarian settings with a

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:32.819
mental health problem, access services.

00:04:32.819 --> 00:04:36.191
We also know that outside of
the formal services system,

00:04:36.191 --> 00:04:40.350
a lot of the community and
informal supports and protective

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:43.800
practices are disrupted in
emergencies and when people are

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:44.670
displaced as well,

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:48.213
which further increases the
risk of mental health issues.

00:04:50.351 --> 00:04:53.100
So I thought it would be helpful
to provide a little bit of

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:57.570
a historical context here
because I think it highlights

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:00.600
where the field has been going
and and where we are now and

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.610
where we need to be going in the future.

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:06.270
So it really wasn't until the
late 1980s that humanitarian

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:08.970
actors first convened, at least on record,

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:12.990
to discuss mental health needs
in the humanitarian context.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:16.680
And this happened in a
refugee camp in Thailand with

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:17.948
refugees from Cambodia,

00:05:17.948 --> 00:05:20.370
where according to these
humanitarian actors,

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.556
they noticed a deteriorating
social condition that needed to

00:05:23.556 --> 00:05:26.380
be addressed within this context.

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:28.800
Move forward one decade,

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:31.977
and we have the first evidence
of psychosocial interventions

00:05:31.977 --> 00:05:35.730
being applied in a humanitarian
setting in the context of

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:37.495
the conflict in the Balkans.

00:05:37.495 --> 00:05:41.317
And then it wasn't until 2007
to 2009 that we started to get

00:05:41.317 --> 00:05:45.706
guidelines and really formalized
recommendations around how

00:05:45.706 --> 00:05:48.417
to respond to the mental health
and psychosocial needs in

00:05:48.417 --> 00:05:50.315
humanitarian settings.

00:05:50.315 --> 00:05:53.460
The publication of the
inter-agency standing committee

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.460
guidelines on mental health
and psychosocial support.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.268
We also saw that during this time,

00:05:58.268 --> 00:06:01.083
UNHCR added mental health conditions

00:06:01.083 --> 00:06:04.620
to their list of kind of,

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:06.300
or the list of conditions they capture

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:08.301
as part of the health information system.

00:06:08.301 --> 00:06:10.020
Prior to 2009,

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:13.410
there was essentially no record
of people seeking support

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:15.986
for mental health through
the primary care system.

00:06:15.986 --> 00:06:18.180
And then in the 2010s,

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:21.210
we started getting information
and guidelines around how to

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:23.498
integrate mental health
psychosocial support,

00:06:23.498 --> 00:06:25.937
which I'll start referring
to as MHPSS because it's a

00:06:25.937 --> 00:06:30.660
mouthful across different sectors
in protection services and

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.643
primary care in other settings.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.720
So since 2010, there's really also been

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:38.280
this proliferation in research on

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:40.463
mental health or MHPSS

00:06:40.463 --> 00:06:42.660
as you can see here to the right.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.090
These are the publications
in PubMed that include

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:48.330
mental health and humanitarian settings.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:51.390
And you see this rapid increase
after about 2010 in the

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:54.025
amount of research being
conducted on this topic.

00:06:54.025 --> 00:06:56.587
In parallel, we've also
seen the development of

00:06:56.587 --> 00:06:59.846
various resources to support
the implementation of

00:06:59.846 --> 00:07:02.714
MHPSS in emergencies.

00:07:02.714 --> 00:07:04.327
WHO has invested a lot

00:07:04.327 --> 00:07:08.040
in developing these scalable
psychological interventions,

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:10.260
which are manualized interventions,

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:13.560
evidence-based interventions
that can be delivered by

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:16.320
non-specialists in order to
address that gap I mentioned

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:19.276
between access to specialized
mental health providers

00:07:19.276 --> 00:07:21.693
in these contexts.

00:07:23.138 --> 00:07:26.460
And I think the good news
here is that we have effective

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:27.870
mental health interventions.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:31.770
This forest plot on the upper
right hand side of the screen

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:32.930
is the result of,

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:36.120
or are the results from an
umbrella review or a systematic

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:40.143
review of reviews where
they've essentially found that

00:07:40.143 --> 00:07:40.976
in humanitarian settings,

00:07:40.976 --> 00:07:43.440
we have pretty good
interventions that seem to work,

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:47.040
particularly in adults to
address issues like depressive

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:48.266
symptoms, anxiety symptoms,

00:07:48.266 --> 00:07:52.265
post-traumatic stress
symptoms and distress.

00:07:52.265 --> 00:07:54.281
There's also been a lot of
investment in implementation

00:07:54.281 --> 00:07:58.020
science and trying to understand
how can we overcome some of

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:01.286
the barriers that are exist
to delivering and accessing

00:08:01.286 --> 00:08:05.760
mental health and psychosocial
support in emergencies.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:07.625
So all of this is very good,

00:08:07.625 --> 00:08:09.810
but one of the things that
I've been thinking a lot about

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:12.360
and that I think motivates some
of the work I'll share with

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.913
you today is why haven't we
seen this evidence really being

00:08:15.913 --> 00:08:19.020
translated into population level impact?

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:23.403
Why is it not being adopted
within the humanitarian sector?

00:08:23.403 --> 00:08:26.370
And I think there are a few
reasons why we haven't yet seen

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:29.274
kind of the translation of
this evidence into improvements

00:08:29.274 --> 00:08:32.340
in population level wellbeing.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:34.680
The first is that many of
the interventions we have,

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.090
there are effects are short-lived.

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:39.510
So often we see that people,
when they're in say a,

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:41.850
group-based intervention over eight weeks,

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:45.420
they tend to improve, but after
then it intervention ends.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:47.760
They often return toward, maybe not fully,

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:51.135
but regress a bit as far as
their mental health conditions.

00:08:51.135 --> 00:08:54.297
We also know that evidence-based
interventions are often not

00:08:54.297 --> 00:08:57.690
adopted or sustained
within routine services.

00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:00.480
So a lot of the evidence is
generated within the context of

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:03.690
research and it's challenging
for various reasons to

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:08.690
actually integrate that into
routine care delivery systems.

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:10.290
And then the third point here,

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:13.020
which I think is really
central to my talk today,

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:16.470
is that many of these interventions
really do not consider

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:17.950
the contextual, structural,

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:20.400
or social determinants of mental health,

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:23.970
which we know are so important to people's

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:25.593
lived experience and wellbeing.

00:09:26.806 --> 00:09:30.030
So this is why I present
to you what I think is the

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:32.400
rationale for multi-sectoral
interventions.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.100
And what I mean by that is, first,

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.080
we know that mental
health is a central part

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:39.060
of people's lived experience.

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:41.220
We also know that it
exists within the context

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:42.420
of many other factors.

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:47.216
And these are just a list
of of social and structural

00:09:47.216 --> 00:09:49.166
determinants of health and mental health

00:09:49.166 --> 00:09:50.880
that we see in the literature,

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.661
but also from some of our
formative work that relate to the

00:09:53.661 --> 00:09:57.983
mental health and wellbeing
forcibly displaced persons.

00:09:57.983 --> 00:10:00.348
But if we think about mental health as,

00:10:00.348 --> 00:10:04.230
as operating in a vacuum by
itself and ignore these root

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:07.116
causes and these risk factors
for mental health problems,

00:10:07.116 --> 00:10:10.290
how would we expect perhaps
that the intervention effects

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:13.782
that we're trying to to
to test can be sustained?

00:10:13.782 --> 00:10:18.782
So we did a survey with 225
refugee and migrant women in

00:10:19.114 --> 00:10:22.050
Ecuador and Panama trying to understand

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:25.800
what are the factors of
your lived experience

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:27.510
that relate most strongly to your

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:30.062
overall wellbeing and life satisfaction?

00:10:30.062 --> 00:10:33.360
And what we essentially found
was that people's sense of

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:36.990
safety and their standard
of living were most strongly

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:40.680
correlated with their overall
wellbeing and quality of life.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:43.261
This probably isn't so
surprising for many people,

00:10:43.261 --> 00:10:45.755
but what I think is important
to note is that these are not

00:10:45.755 --> 00:10:48.180
usually the focus of our mental health

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:50.010
and psychosocial interventions.

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:52.028
Often our interventions are
focused very much on our

00:10:52.028 --> 00:10:54.720
thoughts and our feelings, our behaviors,

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:56.347
our interpersonal relationships,

00:10:56.347 --> 00:10:59.616
but if really at the root of
people's wellbeing is their

00:10:59.616 --> 00:11:03.240
sense of safety and protection
and their standard of living,

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:06.120
are we missing the mark by
thinking kind of too narrowly

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:09.690
around what's happening immediately
in people's minds and in

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:12.423
their kind of immediate
circles and relationships,

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:15.540
not to undermine or or discount those,

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:18.360
there are important a aspects
of of people's wellbeing,

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:20.550
but I think what I'd
like to argue today is

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:22.350
we need to think a bit more broadly.

00:11:23.372 --> 00:11:27.930
And so part of that is
highlighted in this figure here.

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:28.770
So if people,

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:31.020
if we are able to address
people's mental health with these

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:33.243
interventions that I've
shown you that are effective,

00:11:33.243 --> 00:11:36.235
that people are operating and
returning to context where all

00:11:36.235 --> 00:11:38.565
of these risk factors
continue to be present,

00:11:38.565 --> 00:11:41.730
can we really expect
that those mental health

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:44.048
effects will be long lasting?

00:11:44.048 --> 00:11:47.219
So I'd like to start today
just with an example of a study

00:11:47.219 --> 00:11:52.080
that we did a while ago now
that looked at this intersection

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:55.440
of intimate partner violence
and mental health in Nyarugusu

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:57.123
refugee camp in Tanzania.

00:11:57.976 --> 00:12:02.730
So Nyarugusu is a refugee camp
in western Tanzania that was

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:05.160
established in the mid 1990s.

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:07.350
At the time we started the study in 2014,

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:12.030
there were 65,000 registered
refugees living in the camp,

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:14.248
primarily from the Eastern DRC.

00:12:14.248 --> 00:12:16.263
And just a little bit of context here,

00:12:16.263 --> 00:12:19.399
refugees who are living in
Nyarugusu do have really a lot of

00:12:19.399 --> 00:12:22.590
restrictions as far as their mobility.

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:24.312
They're not allowed to leave the camp,

00:12:24.312 --> 00:12:26.100
they also are not able to work.

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:27.180
There are a lot of restrictions

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:28.773
that can't get formal employment.

00:12:28.773 --> 00:12:30.930
A lot of their livelihood opportunities

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:32.580
are really restricted.

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:34.375
And I think that's important
context when we think about

00:12:34.375 --> 00:12:37.608
their risk for both intimate
partner violence and also also

00:12:37.608 --> 00:12:39.000
mental health conditions.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:41.911
And we'll see how that
plays out in just a minute.

00:12:41.911 --> 00:12:44.700
So within this context,

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:46.978
intimate partner violence
or IPV is one of the main

00:12:46.978 --> 00:12:50.100
protection priority priorities recognized

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:52.470
by UNHCR in Nyarugusu.

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:55.478
It's well kind of that it's
a prevalent public health

00:12:55.478 --> 00:13:00.381
condition affecting many of
the people in this setting.

00:13:00.381 --> 00:13:04.080
We also know based on
data from the camp itself,

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:06.530
but also some more kind
of rigorous data from

00:13:06.530 --> 00:13:08.520
population-based surveys,

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:12.084
surveys in the eastern DRC
where most of the refugees are

00:13:12.084 --> 00:13:15.613
from that IPV is one of the
leading risk factors for poor

00:13:15.613 --> 00:13:19.380
mental health among women
of reproductive age.

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:22.350
So here's some data from
this household based

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:24.720
population-based survey
that was conducted in

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:26.970
the Eastern DRC that
shows essentially that

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:30.808
women with a history of IPV have roughly

00:13:30.808 --> 00:13:34.950
a twofold increase likelihood
of having depression,

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:39.210
PTSD and suicidal ideation
or lifetime attempts.

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:42.240
We don't see the same pattern
for substance use, however,

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:44.760
I think it's pretty consistent
across a lot of these other

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:48.180
common mental disorders to
see that there's an increased

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:50.283
risk here associated with IPV.

00:13:52.005 --> 00:13:53.820
So within Nyarugusu,

00:13:53.820 --> 00:13:56.908
the approach to addressing
these related conditions

00:13:56.908 --> 00:13:58.530
is pretty siloed.

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:01.770
So on the one hand we
have the protection actors

00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:04.806
and at the time it was the
International Rescue Committee

00:14:04.806 --> 00:14:07.650
managing a lot of gender-based
violence prevention and

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:10.920
response programs to address IPV.

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:14.010
And then we also had some
health actors focused on

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:16.274
addressing the mental health
and psychosocial support needs

00:14:16.274 --> 00:14:19.216
through the Tanzania Red Cross.

00:14:19.216 --> 00:14:22.350
These services were
separated at multiple levels,

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:25.830
so they were largely siloed
and not very well coordinated.

00:14:25.830 --> 00:14:29.040
And so essentially what this
means is if say there's a

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:31.980
survivor of IPV with mental
health needs and she goes to

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:34.590
the Red Cross to get mental health care,

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:37.153
those services are often
not very well coordinated to

00:14:37.153 --> 00:14:41.553
address the GBV or IPV needs as well.

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:45.870
So our goal with this study
was to see can we test an

00:14:45.870 --> 00:14:49.170
integrated intervention that
simultaneously aims to address

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:51.810
intimate partner violence
and mental health in order to

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:54.720
improve kind of the
synergies between these two

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:56.433
multi-sectoral approaches?

00:14:57.790 --> 00:15:01.617
We started off this study by
doing some formative research

00:15:01.617 --> 00:15:04.470
to understand what are the
salient mental health and

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:07.260
psychosocial problems affecting survivors

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:09.506
of IPV in Nyarugusu.

00:15:09.506 --> 00:15:11.769
And we use two methods. We
use free listing interviews,

00:15:11.769 --> 00:15:14.550
which are a rapid qualitative method,

00:15:14.550 --> 00:15:18.334
often used to identify a list
of concepts and prioritize a

00:15:18.334 --> 00:15:21.186
list of concepts or
problems in a community,

00:15:21.186 --> 00:15:24.780
followed by some in-depth came
format interviews to better

00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:28.110
kind of flesh out and describe
the priority mental health

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:31.740
problems that we identified
as affecting IPV survivors in

00:15:31.740 --> 00:15:33.438
this context.

00:15:33.438 --> 00:15:37.140
So the results of this formative
research process revealed

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:40.335
really three key mental health
and psychosocial problems.

00:15:40.335 --> 00:15:43.830
The first was stress or too many thoughts.

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:46.011
In Swahili it's msongo wa mawazo.

00:15:46.011 --> 00:15:48.150
Which was really described as those

00:15:48.150 --> 00:15:50.597
internalized feelings of distress.

00:15:50.597 --> 00:15:54.540
And then we had this concept
of huzuni or deep sadness,

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:56.471
which was more of this
externalized presentation of

00:15:56.471 --> 00:16:00.518
distress. So feeling really
angry or aggressive or upset.

00:16:00.518 --> 00:16:03.652
And the third mental health
problem that people described as

00:16:03.652 --> 00:16:08.652
affecting survivors of
IPV was fear or hofu,

00:16:09.090 --> 00:16:12.020
which was characterized by worry,

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:14.250
trembling or fearing that something bad

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:15.550
was going to happen again.

00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:18.570
And so these data were really
helpful for us because we were

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:21.887
thinking within the context
of an integrated intervention,

00:16:21.887 --> 00:16:24.735
perhaps the mental health
component can focus on these three

00:16:24.735 --> 00:16:27.843
priority mental health conditions.

00:16:28.926 --> 00:16:32.130
The other part of our formative
research and a really key

00:16:32.130 --> 00:16:34.650
aspect of this multi-sectorial
process was thinking about

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:38.370
where to position this type
of multi-sectoral intervention

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:41.160
within the refugee camp setting.

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:43.650
And we went through a
process of community mapping,

00:16:43.650 --> 00:16:46.110
which first involved
like physically mapping,

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:48.519
who are the stakeholders
involved in these two activities

00:16:48.519 --> 00:16:52.622
and MHPSS support and GBV
response and where are they

00:16:52.622 --> 00:16:55.650
physically located in the camp,

00:16:55.650 --> 00:16:58.020
which is the map on the left hand side.

00:16:58.020 --> 00:17:01.770
And then we also went through
a process of doing some focus

00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:04.680
groups with people in the
community, with community leaders,

00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:07.599
with humanitarian practitioners
to understand how people

00:17:07.599 --> 00:17:12.599
navigate these different
systems and and services and

00:17:13.650 --> 00:17:15.428
that are available to them.

00:17:15.428 --> 00:17:17.160
And what I think is really interesting,

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:20.040
what was super helpful and
critical about this process for

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:22.770
us, as you can see on the
right hand side in blue,

00:17:22.770 --> 00:17:25.860
are those kind of formal actors
or the formal system where

00:17:25.860 --> 00:17:29.157
you have the protection providers
that do the GBV response

00:17:29.157 --> 00:17:32.070
and and prevention programs like the IRC,

00:17:32.070 --> 00:17:34.680
you have the police,
you have case managers,

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:37.125
legal services and health services.

00:17:37.125 --> 00:17:40.560
But what we didn't discover
until we did those focus groups,

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:45.060
or even from talking after
talking with the humanitarian

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:49.713
actors, we didn't yet
know of this entirely,

00:17:50.611 --> 00:17:52.737
this informal system.

00:17:52.737 --> 00:17:56.040
And so essentially why
this is important is that

00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:57.840
for IPV survivors,

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:00.825
it's expected and really only
culturally acceptable for that

00:18:00.825 --> 00:18:04.320
problem initially to
be aimed to be resolved

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:05.730
within the family.

00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:06.728
And so it's really looked down upon

00:18:06.728 --> 00:18:08.520
and there's a lot of stigma

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:11.790
associated with going directly
to that formal system.

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:14.222
So most of the survivors of
IPV are still operating within

00:18:14.222 --> 00:18:17.759
this informal system of
support through the family.

00:18:17.759 --> 00:18:21.300
And only if the family's not
able to resolve the issue,

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:23.100
can they start engaging with neighbors,

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:25.290
with the local police
force, with village leaders,

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:26.784
et cetera, et cetera.

00:18:26.784 --> 00:18:27.930
So for us,

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:30.480
why this was important is
that if we only thought about

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:33.450
reaching women within the
foremost system of care,

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:36.060
we would have missed a lot of
people who could potentially

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:38.730
benefit from an integrated mental health

00:18:38.730 --> 00:18:40.413
and IPV intervention.

00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:46.500
- But the fact that they go
first or try to resolve it

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:47.610
into the family,

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:51.058
isn't that you also
because of the availability

00:18:51.058 --> 00:18:54.558
of the other more formal services or

00:18:54.558 --> 00:18:58.111
more on a continuous basis?

00:18:58.111 --> 00:18:59.580
Accessibility as well.

00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:01.110
- It's a great point.

00:19:01.110 --> 00:19:02.100
Yes, certainly.

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:04.890
I mean there the access
to services specifically

00:19:04.890 --> 00:19:06.403
access to justice was really limited.

00:19:06.403 --> 00:19:09.900
And so the use of these local
courts and village leaders was

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:11.597
really essential for that purpose.

00:19:11.597 --> 00:19:14.734
The way it was described to us
primarily was related to the

00:19:14.734 --> 00:19:17.070
stigma with actually seeking services

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:18.150
through the formal system.

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:21.470
There were social consequences
of doing that unless it was

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:24.450
kind of the permitted,

00:19:24.450 --> 00:19:27.060
I'd say by some of these informal actors.

00:19:27.060 --> 00:19:29.220
But certainly access and availability

00:19:29.220 --> 00:19:30.903
is another challenge as well.

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:33.770
Thanks for that question.

00:19:34.754 --> 00:19:36.300
So with that information,

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:39.173
we designed what we called
the Nguvu intervention,

00:19:39.173 --> 00:19:42.360
and Nguvu is a Swahili
term for power or strength.

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:44.966
It was an eight session
intervention that combined cognitive

00:19:44.966 --> 00:19:46.410
processing therapy,

00:19:46.410 --> 00:19:50.070
which is a psychological
intervention designed for survivors

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:53.220
of trauma that fit nicely
with those problem areas

00:19:53.220 --> 00:19:54.207
we identified in that in

00:19:54.207 --> 00:19:56.820
the formative phase of the research.

00:19:56.820 --> 00:19:59.100
And we combined cognitive
processing therapy,

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:02.100
which I should also mention
had been previously tested in

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:03.030
the eastern DRC.

00:20:03.030 --> 00:20:05.786
So there was some already
cultural adaptation that was done

00:20:05.786 --> 00:20:08.088
that we felt needed
appropriate intervention

00:20:08.088 --> 00:20:10.089
for this context.

00:20:10.089 --> 00:20:12.390
And we combined that
with advocacy counseling,

00:20:12.390 --> 00:20:15.390
which is an intervention that
has some evidence for reducing

00:20:15.390 --> 00:20:18.750
kind of the chronicity and
intensity of IPV among women

00:20:18.750 --> 00:20:22.957
who've already experienced
some form of domestic violence.

00:20:22.957 --> 00:20:24.960
As far as the implementation,

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:26.820
this photo's a picture of our research

00:20:26.820 --> 00:20:28.830
and our intervention counselors,

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:31.500
so our research assistants
and intervention counselors in

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:34.142
Nyarugusu after one of our trainings.

00:20:34.142 --> 00:20:37.740
So we used this task sharing
approach where we hired a team

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:42.060
of volunteers from the
community or incentive workers,

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:45.300
they were given a very small
incentive to work with the

00:20:45.300 --> 00:20:46.830
International rescue committee to

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:50.496
do some community outreach
as part of the GBV response.

00:20:50.496 --> 00:20:53.490
And we worked with this cadre
of women and trained them in

00:20:53.490 --> 00:20:56.730
this intervention so that they
could provide it within these

00:20:56.730 --> 00:21:00.030
community settings kind
of integrated within IRCs,

00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:01.653
broader GBV program.

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:05.431
The intervention itself
involved one individual session

00:21:05.431 --> 00:21:08.910
followed by seven group
sessions, and again,

00:21:08.910 --> 00:21:11.970
we integrated it within
IRCs Women's Protection and

00:21:11.970 --> 00:21:15.420
Empowerment program with the
intention of kind of fitting it

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:19.170
within an existing system of
care that women were already

00:21:19.170 --> 00:21:21.150
comfortable participating in.

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:23.463
So the women's protection and
empowerment program was not

00:21:23.463 --> 00:21:25.590
specific to GBV, it was a,

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:27.750
a service that was provided to women.

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:30.311
It was not a stigmatizing place
for women to go and if they

00:21:30.311 --> 00:21:31.740
participated there,

00:21:31.740 --> 00:21:34.089
they wouldn't necessarily be
identified as someone who was a

00:21:34.089 --> 00:21:36.039
survivor of IPV,

00:21:36.039 --> 00:21:40.579
which was a a key
protection concern for us.

00:21:40.579 --> 00:21:41.589
And then finally,

00:21:41.589 --> 00:21:44.329
going back to our discussion
around the informal versus

00:21:44.329 --> 00:21:46.140
formal systems of care,

00:21:46.140 --> 00:21:50.100
we ended up recruiting women
through community-based women's

00:21:50.100 --> 00:21:52.290
groups so that we could reach a broader

00:21:52.290 --> 00:21:55.770
number of women from community
settings instead of relying

00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:58.860
just on those who were
accessing services through those

00:21:58.860 --> 00:21:59.943
formal channels.

00:22:01.621 --> 00:22:03.234
As far as our study design,

00:22:03.234 --> 00:22:06.195
we conducted a cluster randomized
trial where we randomized

00:22:06.195 --> 00:22:10.650
those women's groups from
which we recruited to receive

00:22:10.650 --> 00:22:14.397
these in Nguvu intervention
or to receive IRCs usual kind

00:22:14.397 --> 00:22:18.021
of care program for GBV survivors.

00:22:18.021 --> 00:22:23.021
And we were specifically looking
to enroll adult Congolese

00:22:24.090 --> 00:22:28.110
refugee women in Nyarugusu
who reported past year

00:22:28.110 --> 00:22:31.054
experiences of IPV and moderate to severe

00:22:31.054 --> 00:22:33.441
psychological distress.

00:22:33.441 --> 00:22:36.780
The outcomes that we measured at baseline

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:38.880
and then one week after the intervention,

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:43.530
so that's about a 10 week period
was were anxiety symptoms,

00:22:43.530 --> 00:22:44.970
depressive symptoms,

00:22:44.970 --> 00:22:47.014
symptoms of post-traumatic
stress disorder,

00:22:47.014 --> 00:22:48.930
and then the frequency of physical,

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:51.483
psychological or sexual IPV.

00:22:53.724 --> 00:22:56.100
So just to characterize
this sample at baseline,

00:22:56.100 --> 00:22:59.490
we ended up enrolling
311 women in the study.

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:02.150
They were about 33 years of age on average

00:23:02.150 --> 00:23:05.700
had lived in Nyarugusu
for 17 years on average.

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:09.630
This is really a protracted
emergency setting.

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:11.340
A lot of our research team in fact

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:13.410
had been born within this camp.

00:23:13.410 --> 00:23:16.010
A lot of people had lived
most of their lives there.

00:23:16.935 --> 00:23:19.950
Two-thirds of the women in
our sample were married and

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:22.457
living with their current partner.

00:23:22.457 --> 00:23:24.240
And as far as their mental health,

00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:27.420
this profile is largely by
design because they were required

00:23:27.420 --> 00:23:28.830
to have at least moderate symptoms

00:23:28.830 --> 00:23:30.686
of distress in order to participate.

00:23:30.686 --> 00:23:34.440
But you can see kind of pretty
similar levels of anxiety,

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:38.160
depressive and PTSD symptoms
that are between a moderate

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:41.356
amount and a lot of symptoms
over the past two weeks.

00:23:41.356 --> 00:23:46.356
And what I think is really
troubling but important is just

00:23:46.444 --> 00:23:50.930
looking at the profiles of IPV as well.

00:23:50.930 --> 00:23:53.538
So the requirement for being
eligible for the study is that

00:23:53.538 --> 00:23:56.247
they reported having experienced some form

00:23:56.247 --> 00:23:58.531
of IPV within the past year.

00:23:58.531 --> 00:23:59.910
But as you can see,

00:23:59.910 --> 00:24:01.620
the majority of women who participated

00:24:01.620 --> 00:24:03.570
reported had been experienced sexual,

00:24:03.570 --> 00:24:08.040
physical and psychological
IPV within the past year.

00:24:08.040 --> 00:24:11.220
And it was actually quite
rare that women reported not

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:12.960
having experienced sexual violence

00:24:12.960 --> 00:24:15.213
perpetrated by an intimate partner.

00:24:17.910 --> 00:24:18.983
As far as our outcome.

00:24:18.983 --> 00:24:22.050
So here we conducted mixed
effects models to look at the

00:24:22.050 --> 00:24:24.852
difference in change from
baseline to follow up between our

00:24:24.852 --> 00:24:27.280
Nguvu condition and those who received

00:24:27.280 --> 00:24:29.459
or were referred to usual care.

00:24:29.459 --> 00:24:32.100
And what we ended up seeing this,

00:24:32.100 --> 00:24:34.525
these are just effect sizes
to kind of visualize the the

00:24:34.525 --> 00:24:37.097
changes over time.

00:24:37.097 --> 00:24:40.620
We did see that women who
participated in the in Nguvu

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:43.516
intervention showed significantly
greater reductions in

00:24:43.516 --> 00:24:47.182
depression, anxiety and PTSD symptoms.

00:24:47.182 --> 00:24:50.580
We did not see any change or
any significant change as far

00:24:50.580 --> 00:24:52.680
as IPV between the usual care

00:24:52.680 --> 00:24:54.963
and the and Nguvu condition.

00:24:57.037 --> 00:24:59.850
I did just wanna go on a brief
tangent about measurement of

00:24:59.850 --> 00:25:02.070
mental health because I think
it is important and actually

00:25:02.070 --> 00:25:05.460
one of the most surprising and
interesting findings for me

00:25:05.460 --> 00:25:07.512
is part of this study.

00:25:07.512 --> 00:25:10.200
So one of the things that I
mentioned from the beginning is

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:12.976
after having gone through that
formative research process,

00:25:12.976 --> 00:25:17.976
we decided that those
three concepts of distress,

00:25:18.175 --> 00:25:22.110
hofu, msongo wa mawazo, and huzuni

00:25:22.110 --> 00:25:27.110
looked roughly like anxiety
depressive PTSD symptoms.

00:25:27.270 --> 00:25:31.230
And so we perhaps naively
selected some research tools to

00:25:31.230 --> 00:25:34.650
measure our outcomes that
corresponded to depression,

00:25:34.650 --> 00:25:36.913
anxiety and PTSD.

00:25:36.913 --> 00:25:41.274
And so what we did after the
fact is we wanted to say, okay,

00:25:41.274 --> 00:25:45.690
let's look and see how
our data cluster together.

00:25:45.690 --> 00:25:46.590
What are the factors,

00:25:46.590 --> 00:25:49.470
the underlying mental health
conditions and how these

00:25:49.470 --> 00:25:52.290
symptoms of mental health problems

00:25:52.290 --> 00:25:54.210
correlate with one another.

00:25:54.210 --> 00:25:57.840
So what I'm showing you on
this slide are the items.

00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:00.300
So each of those words
corresponds to an item on one of

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:04.140
those measures, the gray items
corresponding to depression,

00:26:04.140 --> 00:26:07.714
red for anxiety, purple for PTSD.

00:26:07.714 --> 00:26:11.012
And if we did kind of looked
at the factor structure and if

00:26:11.012 --> 00:26:16.012
these three conditions
were valid in this context,

00:26:16.170 --> 00:26:18.600
what we'd expect to see is
that the depression items would

00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:20.926
be more correlated or
clustered with one another.

00:26:20.926 --> 00:26:23.986
Similarly with the anxiety
and with the PTSD items.

00:26:23.986 --> 00:26:27.660
So we did an exploratory factor
analysis and what we ended

00:26:27.660 --> 00:26:29.910
up finding was something more like this,

00:26:29.910 --> 00:26:33.650
it's like random mixing of
symptoms across these three

00:26:33.650 --> 00:26:36.000
conditions that no longer looked like

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:39.491
depression, anxiety and PTSD.

00:26:39.491 --> 00:26:42.000
So at first we sat there
and were kind of stumped.

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:43.500
We didn't know what to make of this.

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:45.330
We tried to say it was just some

00:26:45.330 --> 00:26:47.676
strange statistical issue, who knows.

00:26:47.676 --> 00:26:50.310
But after spending some
time with this data,

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:53.280
what we ended up seeing is
that these problems almost

00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:56.700
perfectly aligned with those
three initial concepts of

00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:59.181
distress that were described
to us by the women we

00:26:59.181 --> 00:27:02.073
interviewed in that formative process.

00:27:02.924 --> 00:27:05.190
So for me, what I take away from this,

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:06.660
I mean we know that mental health

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:08.220
is really difficult to measure.

00:27:08.220 --> 00:27:10.040
It's we don't have gold standards.

00:27:10.040 --> 00:27:13.260
We know there's cultural
variation in how mental health

00:27:13.260 --> 00:27:14.498
conditions present,

00:27:14.498 --> 00:27:17.670
but I think the bigger piece
and the bigger picture here is

00:27:17.670 --> 00:27:21.348
just the importance of
listening and trying to avoid

00:27:21.348 --> 00:27:24.780
applying our perspective and
a worldview that's familiar to

00:27:24.780 --> 00:27:27.720
us on the data that are presented.

00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:30.600
Because I think perhaps
naively we had done that in the

00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:33.237
situation and kind of forced
what we were hearing to fit

00:27:33.237 --> 00:27:36.990
something that was familiar
when in fact I think we saw this

00:27:36.990 --> 00:27:40.380
amazing triangulation of our
qualitative research and our

00:27:40.380 --> 00:27:43.443
quantitative research telling
us something quite different.

00:27:44.835 --> 00:27:49.620
All right, so back to the the
study and the relation with

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:51.684
multi-sectorial interventions.

00:27:51.684 --> 00:27:54.900
I just shared with you the
main results from the study,

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:56.670
but if we pull back the
curtain a little bit,

00:27:56.670 --> 00:27:59.550
as always it was a bit messier.

00:27:59.550 --> 00:28:02.160
There were a lot of
implementation challenges.

00:28:02.160 --> 00:28:04.410
We had a lot of issues
with staffing and kind of

00:28:04.410 --> 00:28:07.650
coordination and organization
and all of these other factors

00:28:07.650 --> 00:28:10.440
that come up in most research projects.

00:28:10.440 --> 00:28:12.533
I hope or not I hope, but I bet.

00:28:12.533 --> 00:28:15.884
And so we took some time to
conduct a process evaluation to

00:28:15.884 --> 00:28:18.455
do some interviews with
different stakeholders who

00:28:18.455 --> 00:28:20.010
participated in the study,

00:28:20.010 --> 00:28:22.727
those who were a bit
external to the program,

00:28:22.727 --> 00:28:24.537
some focus group discussions.

00:28:24.537 --> 00:28:27.390
And then we also collaborated
with a group from

00:28:27.390 --> 00:28:31.110
the Global Health Justice and
Governance program at Columbia

00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:34.770
to do a funding analysis
and look at policy documents

00:28:34.770 --> 00:28:36.620
at the same time the study was conducted

00:28:36.620 --> 00:28:39.599
to try to understand what
factors might have influenced

00:28:39.599 --> 00:28:42.723
the implementation
challenges that we faced.

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:46.170
And what we came up with was a model,

00:28:46.170 --> 00:28:48.000
this is a very simplified version of it,

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:51.060
the full versions in the the
paper that you probably aren't

00:28:51.060 --> 00:28:51.893
able to read,

00:28:51.893 --> 00:28:54.513
but I'm happy to share the
the slides with you after.

00:28:55.770 --> 00:28:58.590
We came up with a model that
identified kind of multi-layer

00:28:58.590 --> 00:29:01.013
determinants that probably
looks very similar to the social

00:29:01.013 --> 00:29:05.085
ecological model for many of
you that influenced the ability

00:29:05.085 --> 00:29:08.670
to integrate our GBV and our
mental health components of

00:29:08.670 --> 00:29:10.320
this intervention.

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:12.934
So just to go through these quickly,

00:29:12.934 --> 00:29:14.520
at the structural level,

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:16.762
I mentioned at the very
beginning some of these complex

00:29:16.762 --> 00:29:21.195
policies related to freedom of movement,

00:29:21.195 --> 00:29:24.856
ability to kind of earn a
living and participate in

00:29:24.856 --> 00:29:28.920
livelihood activities and
this policy landscape and the

00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:32.370
broader context I think really
made it complex for us to

00:29:32.370 --> 00:29:35.568
address these related issues
because in many ways those

00:29:35.568 --> 00:29:39.960
structural factors really
reinforced some of the risks for

00:29:39.960 --> 00:29:44.520
both GBV and mental health problems.

00:29:44.520 --> 00:29:45.353
We also found,

00:29:45.353 --> 00:29:48.450
and related to your earlier
question that in the legal

00:29:48.450 --> 00:29:52.800
system there were few protections
against GBV and access

00:29:52.800 --> 00:29:54.810
to justice was quite limited.

00:29:54.810 --> 00:29:57.870
There was a a legal officer who
had come into the camp a few

00:29:57.870 --> 00:30:00.186
times a week to work with people on cases,

00:30:00.186 --> 00:30:03.810
but it was really expensive.
It took a long time.

00:30:03.810 --> 00:30:06.270
There were a lot of factors
that made it hard for people to

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:09.857
access that type of support if
they they wanted to and felt

00:30:09.857 --> 00:30:13.042
it was acceptable to do so.

00:30:13.042 --> 00:30:16.080
Also, mental health
services were just limited.

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:18.450
It was not prioritized in the camp.

00:30:18.450 --> 00:30:20.190
There weren't a lot of resources available

00:30:20.190 --> 00:30:22.650
to address mental health needs.

00:30:22.650 --> 00:30:25.231
And I think a lot of this was
ultimately really aggravated

00:30:25.231 --> 00:30:27.030
by the funding environment.

00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:30.223
So funding for GBV and mental
health are very separate and

00:30:30.223 --> 00:30:33.645
this made it quite challenging
for us to actually convince

00:30:33.645 --> 00:30:36.870
people funded from the mental
health actors and those funded

00:30:36.870 --> 00:30:40.173
by the GBV funders to work
together and to try to achieve

00:30:40.173 --> 00:30:44.880
kind of shared or respective goals.

00:30:44.880 --> 00:30:46.698
Not to mention that a lot of
the funding requests were not

00:30:46.698 --> 00:30:49.204
fulfilled. So while GBV was prioritized,

00:30:49.204 --> 00:30:54.204
the funding was I think only
40% or the appeal was only 40%

00:30:55.754 --> 00:30:59.130
percent funded and expenditures
were half of what they

00:30:59.130 --> 00:31:00.060
actually expected.

00:31:00.060 --> 00:31:04.233
So really under-resourced as
far as the projected needs.

00:31:06.113 --> 00:31:08.490
Another challenge at
the next level was just

00:31:08.490 --> 00:31:10.230
interinstitutional coordination.

00:31:10.230 --> 00:31:12.750
And I think this is a huge
challenge in the humanitarian

00:31:12.750 --> 00:31:16.172
sector in general, just
coordination across the board.

00:31:16.172 --> 00:31:17.635
But surprisingly,

00:31:17.635 --> 00:31:20.814
even between MHPSS and GBD
actors coordination was

00:31:20.814 --> 00:31:23.400
very limited.

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:26.877
We found that there were
working groups operating in

00:31:26.877 --> 00:31:30.405
the MHPSS space and the GBD
space who would say we know the

00:31:30.405 --> 00:31:32.280
importance of the other,

00:31:32.280 --> 00:31:34.380
we know that this is
related to what we're doing,

00:31:34.380 --> 00:31:38.160
but there was very little actual
talking or no joint action

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:40.310
plans about how these two sectors

00:31:40.310 --> 00:31:42.243
of two areas would work together.

00:31:43.470 --> 00:31:45.793
We also found, found
that within the GBV plan,

00:31:45.793 --> 00:31:48.900
it included an element
of psychosocial support,

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:51.480
but not really any
operationalization of what that was.

00:31:51.480 --> 00:31:54.750
It was just this mandate or
this recommendation I should

00:31:54.750 --> 00:31:57.112
say, to provide psychosocial
support but with very little

00:31:57.112 --> 00:31:59.853
guidance on how to do that.

00:32:00.780 --> 00:32:03.540
And similarly, within
the MHPSS working group,

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:07.776
which was designed to be
multi-sectorial or cross-sectoral,

00:32:07.776 --> 00:32:10.890
the actors involved were largely
working within the health

00:32:10.890 --> 00:32:14.043
system as opposed to engaging
with the protection sector.

00:32:15.420 --> 00:32:16.530
I don't know how to solve this one,

00:32:16.530 --> 00:32:19.180
but if people have ideas,
I'd love love to hear them.

00:32:20.640 --> 00:32:21.473
So at the next level,

00:32:21.473 --> 00:32:23.400
we have the inter-institutional context.

00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:26.530
And this is where I think we
most acutely saw some of our

00:32:26.530 --> 00:32:30.330
challenges within the
organization we were working with.

00:32:30.330 --> 00:32:33.330
There was a really high rate
of staff turnover at all levels

00:32:33.330 --> 00:32:35.660
from headquarters down
to the field staff level.

00:32:35.660 --> 00:32:39.060
And this made it challenging
for us to have kind of reliable

00:32:39.060 --> 00:32:42.060
engagement and buy-in from
our partners because it varied

00:32:42.060 --> 00:32:45.002
based on whoever was the
decision maker at that point.

00:32:45.002 --> 00:32:47.700
And I think ultimately made
it really difficult for our

00:32:47.700 --> 00:32:51.420
counselors to know who they
were accountable to or what they

00:32:51.420 --> 00:32:52.936
were accountable to.

00:32:52.936 --> 00:32:55.350
Another challenge is, I mentioned earlier,

00:32:55.350 --> 00:32:58.980
is that given the siloed
funding and the project-based

00:32:58.980 --> 00:33:01.131
funding that exists in
the humanitarian sector,

00:33:01.131 --> 00:33:05.962
a lot of or the recognition
that it made sense to integrate

00:33:05.962 --> 00:33:08.896
mental health and GBV was
often kind of overcome by the

00:33:08.896 --> 00:33:13.503
need to be accountable to
whatever the objectives were of a,

00:33:13.503 --> 00:33:15.582
of a donor funded program.

00:33:15.582 --> 00:33:17.910
And so there was just a lot
of competing priorities and

00:33:17.910 --> 00:33:20.430
challenges in terms of adopting
and being more flexible

00:33:20.430 --> 00:33:22.473
about this intersectorial approach.

00:33:24.074 --> 00:33:26.276
And then finally, at the
community level, I think

00:33:26.276 --> 00:33:31.113
the main takeaway for me is
that women who participated and

00:33:31.113 --> 00:33:34.680
the community more broadly
really recognized the value of

00:33:34.680 --> 00:33:36.954
bringing this intervention
together that addressed what they

00:33:36.954 --> 00:33:39.650
referred to as their home environment,

00:33:39.650 --> 00:33:43.170
their interpersonal relationships
as well as their minds or

00:33:43.170 --> 00:33:44.318
their mental health.

00:33:44.318 --> 00:33:46.680
But there was this broad
agreement that it was really

00:33:46.680 --> 00:33:48.480
insufficient to actually address

00:33:48.480 --> 00:33:50.340
the root causes of violence.

00:33:50.340 --> 00:33:52.688
And many recommendations came up around

00:33:52.688 --> 00:33:55.893
providing services to men, engaging,

00:33:55.893 --> 00:33:58.830
incorporating livelihood opportunities

00:33:58.830 --> 00:34:00.573
and other factors as well.

00:34:01.689 --> 00:34:04.560
So some of my key takeaways
from this project,

00:34:04.560 --> 00:34:07.080
which is a really formative
learning experience for me was

00:34:07.080 --> 00:34:10.277
just the importance of
prioritizing a prevention approach,

00:34:10.277 --> 00:34:13.378
making sure that we're
incorporating considerations for the

00:34:13.378 --> 00:34:16.620
social and contextual
determinants of mental health

00:34:16.620 --> 00:34:21.030
and of GBV and also making sure
that we are from the outset

00:34:21.030 --> 00:34:22.740
promoting engagement and ownership.

00:34:22.740 --> 00:34:25.680
I think that is so central
to kind of success in any of

00:34:25.680 --> 00:34:28.948
these more complicated types
of interventions and projects.

00:34:28.948 --> 00:34:32.119
So just before I wrap up,
I wanted to highlight a,

00:34:32.119 --> 00:34:35.347
just a couple of ongoing
projects that are hopefully

00:34:35.347 --> 00:34:37.561
building on some of our lessons learned

00:34:37.561 --> 00:34:40.710
from the Nguvu program.

00:34:40.710 --> 00:34:43.750
So over the past three or four
years I've been working with

00:34:43.750 --> 00:34:47.277
HIAs, a humanitarian organization
that is focused on refugee

00:34:47.277 --> 00:34:51.600
protection. They provide, they
have kind of four pillars.

00:34:51.600 --> 00:34:55.350
They provide legal assistance,
they do social protection,

00:34:55.350 --> 00:34:58.247
which includes GBV
prevention and response,

00:34:58.247 --> 00:34:59.850
economic inclusion,

00:34:59.850 --> 00:35:03.042
and also mental health
and psychosocial support.

00:35:03.042 --> 00:35:05.460
And over the past few years
we've been working on some

00:35:05.460 --> 00:35:08.348
projects primarily focused
on providing services for

00:35:08.348 --> 00:35:10.656
Venezuelan migrants.

00:35:10.656 --> 00:35:15.090
As many probably in the room
know the economic and political

00:35:15.090 --> 00:35:18.660
crisis in Venezuela has led
to over 7 million Venezuelans

00:35:18.660 --> 00:35:22.080
fleeing the country and
over 6 million of those have

00:35:22.080 --> 00:35:23.550
remained in Latin America.

00:35:23.550 --> 00:35:27.112
And as you can see here a lot
within Columbia and Peru and

00:35:27.112 --> 00:35:28.263
in Ecuador.

00:35:29.543 --> 00:35:34.251
So our work with HIAs has been
to work along key migration

00:35:34.251 --> 00:35:37.891
route and to evaluate the
effectiveness and implementation

00:35:37.891 --> 00:35:41.820
of community-based
psychosocial and mental health

00:35:41.820 --> 00:35:45.674
interventions that
integrate MHPSS with social

00:35:45.674 --> 00:35:49.769
protection and economic
support for refugees, migrants,

00:35:49.769 --> 00:35:51.750
and also host communities

00:35:51.750 --> 00:35:54.453
in these various sites in Latin America.

00:35:55.422 --> 00:35:58.740
This goes back to this
integrated intervention model and

00:35:58.740 --> 00:36:01.410
just recognizing and here
are some of the common things

00:36:01.410 --> 00:36:04.414
we've heard about in our
formative research around

00:36:04.414 --> 00:36:06.570
the drivers of mental health
problems and what those salient

00:36:06.570 --> 00:36:08.520
mental health problems are.

00:36:08.520 --> 00:36:10.650
But we need to think about
these mental health conditions

00:36:10.650 --> 00:36:14.370
within the context of safety
and security discrimination and

00:36:14.370 --> 00:36:18.307
xenophobia, social connectedness,
social networks and GBV.

00:36:19.632 --> 00:36:22.282
I also wanted to highlight
a few of the mental health

00:36:23.580 --> 00:36:26.250
conditions or constructs that came up

00:36:26.250 --> 00:36:28.080
in a lot of our interviews.

00:36:28.080 --> 00:36:30.219
The first one, malaestar emocional is not

00:36:30.219 --> 00:36:33.510
I think a very direct translation,

00:36:33.510 --> 00:36:36.558
but it's the concept of kind
of a poor state of emotional

00:36:36.558 --> 00:36:39.501
being which came up repeatedly

00:36:39.501 --> 00:36:44.190
in a lot of our different interviews.

00:36:44.190 --> 00:36:46.650
Similarly, sadness has
come up quite a bit,

00:36:46.650 --> 00:36:48.522
as has this other construct
of duelo migratorio

00:36:48.522 --> 00:36:53.522
which is the concept around
migratory grief and mourning.

00:36:53.896 --> 00:36:56.520
And I think these are really
interesting because again,

00:36:56.520 --> 00:36:58.950
a lot of our mental health
interventions are not really

00:36:58.950 --> 00:37:01.920
designed to focus on migration related

00:37:01.920 --> 00:37:04.830
grief and are much more, I guess,

00:37:04.830 --> 00:37:07.440
optimized to address some of
the more clinical mental health

00:37:07.440 --> 00:37:09.693
conditions we're more familiar with.

00:37:11.234 --> 00:37:15.450
So all of these projects
started off during covid

00:37:15.450 --> 00:37:18.179
and so that of course
added another layer to,

00:37:18.179 --> 00:37:21.480
and really exacerbated a lot
of these risk factors for

00:37:21.480 --> 00:37:22.560
mental health problems

00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:25.268
and caused unique forms
of distress as well.

00:37:25.268 --> 00:37:27.900
Here are just some quotes
from some of the qualitative

00:37:27.900 --> 00:37:31.973
interviews we've done with
migrants in Latin America that I

00:37:31.973 --> 00:37:35.520
think really speak to the
connection between mental health

00:37:35.520 --> 00:37:37.143
challenges and the pandemic.

00:37:37.980 --> 00:37:41.400
The first quote is from a mother
who talks about her fear of

00:37:41.400 --> 00:37:44.550
actually catching covid and
what will happen if she herself

00:37:44.550 --> 00:37:46.203
becomes sick and dies,

00:37:46.203 --> 00:37:48.933
who will ultimately take
care of her children.

00:37:50.070 --> 00:37:52.350
The middle quote I think is
really interesting because one

00:37:52.350 --> 00:37:54.610
of the things we found is a lot of

00:37:54.610 --> 00:37:55.770
the supports people have received,

00:37:55.770 --> 00:37:59.250
Venezuelan migrants have received
in Latin America has been

00:37:59.250 --> 00:38:02.640
through community networks
and host communities providing

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:05.433
informal supports to
migrants who are arriving.

00:38:05.433 --> 00:38:08.490
But as this person says,
during the pandemic,

00:38:08.490 --> 00:38:11.340
everyone was really stressed
and worried and people were no

00:38:11.340 --> 00:38:13.050
longer as willing to help because

00:38:13.050 --> 00:38:15.813
of the fear that they
had of catching covid.

00:38:17.100 --> 00:38:19.680
And then the final quote
relates more to this issue of

00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:21.840
livelihoods and work opportunities.

00:38:21.840 --> 00:38:23.790
This comes from a woman in Panama

00:38:23.790 --> 00:38:26.550
who talks about how before or now during

00:38:26.550 --> 00:38:30.540
the pandemic you can't really
move before you could at least

00:38:30.540 --> 00:38:33.780
clean people's houses and now
people are nervous to bring

00:38:33.780 --> 00:38:34.890
strangers into their home.

00:38:34.890 --> 00:38:36.990
And so they've now lost another source

00:38:36.990 --> 00:38:40.255
of livelihoods there as well.

00:38:40.255 --> 00:38:45.255
So one study I wanted just to
highlight is we conducted in

00:38:45.870 --> 00:38:49.680
Panama and in Ecuador where
really the goal here was to

00:38:49.680 --> 00:38:52.497
start off at the very outset
of engaging with communities to

00:38:52.497 --> 00:38:56.210
design mental health and
psychosocial intervention combined

00:38:56.210 --> 00:39:00.450
with a social protection
element to meet the needs of

00:39:00.450 --> 00:39:03.343
refugee migrant and host
community women in three really

00:39:03.343 --> 00:39:06.450
diverse sites in Latin America.

00:39:06.450 --> 00:39:08.970
And we intentionally selected
these three different sites in

00:39:08.970 --> 00:39:11.309
order to understand what is
the role of context as we think

00:39:11.309 --> 00:39:13.408
about implementation and the design

00:39:13.408 --> 00:39:16.475
of mental health interventions.

00:39:16.475 --> 00:39:18.840
The first site in is in Panama,

00:39:18.840 --> 00:39:21.720
so Panama City as well as three
communities that are kind of

00:39:21.720 --> 00:39:25.560
peri-urban communities around
Panama City that where a lot

00:39:25.560 --> 00:39:28.436
of migrants have have moved.

00:39:28.436 --> 00:39:31.170
We also worked in Tulcan, Ecuador,

00:39:31.170 --> 00:39:33.030
which is on the border with Columbia.

00:39:33.030 --> 00:39:36.445
It's a pretty remote rural
town and often a transit point

00:39:36.445 --> 00:39:39.690
for migrants who are moving further south.

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:42.180
And we also worked in Guayaquil, Ecuador,

00:39:42.180 --> 00:39:44.250
which is a large city,

00:39:44.250 --> 00:39:47.711
very urban setting and a
destination for many migrants in

00:39:47.711 --> 00:39:50.703
Ecuador or migrants from Venezuela.

00:39:51.827 --> 00:39:54.761
And as part of this process
we went through a an initial

00:39:54.761 --> 00:39:58.143
participatory intervention design process.

00:39:58.143 --> 00:40:02.190
So we started with the similar
formative research that I

00:40:02.190 --> 00:40:05.220
described in the previous study
where we identified what are

00:40:05.220 --> 00:40:08.790
the most salient outcomes
affecting migrant women in these

00:40:08.790 --> 00:40:13.051
settings and those four
are listed on your right.

00:40:13.051 --> 00:40:15.030
And then we asked people,

00:40:15.030 --> 00:40:18.240
what type of intervention
would you like to see

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:19.470
to address these problems?

00:40:19.470 --> 00:40:22.740
And pretty consistently what
we heard is that women wanted a

00:40:22.740 --> 00:40:24.510
safe space to come together

00:40:24.510 --> 00:40:27.554
as a group to address their needs.

00:40:27.554 --> 00:40:30.060
And the fun part of all of this is kind of

00:40:30.060 --> 00:40:31.110
what happened in the middle.

00:40:31.110 --> 00:40:32.360
And so we said, okay,

00:40:32.360 --> 00:40:35.263
so we have this group
intervention that we want to

00:40:35.263 --> 00:40:38.520
ultimately improve mental
health, improve safety,

00:40:38.520 --> 00:40:40.928
improve connectedness and social support.

00:40:40.928 --> 00:40:43.505
How would an intervention
like this do that?

00:40:43.505 --> 00:40:46.230
And through this really iterative

00:40:46.230 --> 00:40:48.570
and kind of community led process,

00:40:48.570 --> 00:40:53.190
we articulated five key
mechanisms that community members

00:40:53.190 --> 00:40:55.770
felt like would lead to these outcomes.

00:40:55.770 --> 00:40:58.529
And those are the five
mechanisms listed here.

00:40:58.529 --> 00:41:01.832
And then the final step of
this process was to say, okay,

00:41:01.832 --> 00:41:05.460
you want to enhance safety and protection.

00:41:05.460 --> 00:41:06.390
How would you do that?

00:41:06.390 --> 00:41:10.440
What are some activities or
intervention components that we

00:41:10.440 --> 00:41:12.090
can bring into this intervention

00:41:12.090 --> 00:41:15.480
in order to activate that mechanism?

00:41:15.480 --> 00:41:17.850
And the part of this though was
so interesting as we brought

00:41:17.850 --> 00:41:20.960
in components from
evidence-based interventions,

00:41:20.960 --> 00:41:24.106
existing interventions and
kind of hopped those with

00:41:24.106 --> 00:41:28.440
participants but also had
women bring in kind of local

00:41:28.440 --> 00:41:31.440
practices and processes that
they had used or they were

00:41:31.440 --> 00:41:35.310
familiar with in their cultural
context in order to activate

00:41:35.310 --> 00:41:38.250
these mechanisms and
ultimately kind of combined and

00:41:38.250 --> 00:41:42.110
massaged and iterated of those
in order to ultimately arrive

00:41:42.110 --> 00:41:44.933
at the Entre Nosotras intervention.

00:41:44.933 --> 00:41:49.933
Entre Nosotras means among
or between us in Spanish,

00:41:50.580 --> 00:41:53.850
it is a strength-based
intervention that is a five session

00:41:53.850 --> 00:41:56.096
weekly intervention that covers

00:41:56.096 --> 00:41:58.797
the five topics listed on this slide.

00:41:58.797 --> 00:42:00.990
It is delivered by a trained,

00:42:00.990 --> 00:42:02.580
a pair of trained facilitators.

00:42:02.580 --> 00:42:03.660
So community members,

00:42:03.660 --> 00:42:06.270
many of whom were actually
involved in the intervention

00:42:06.270 --> 00:42:09.917
design itself to groups of
women in their communities.

00:42:09.917 --> 00:42:13.578
This was much more of a mental
health promotion and kind of

00:42:13.578 --> 00:42:18.000
protection intervention than
it was more of a clinical one.

00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:20.394
And so we focused this
intervention on women in the

00:42:20.394 --> 00:42:23.460
community who had kind
of low to moderate levels

00:42:23.460 --> 00:42:25.950
of distress as well.

00:42:25.950 --> 00:42:28.590
And we also ensured that this
intervention was adaptable

00:42:28.590 --> 00:42:31.023
across each of our study contexts.

00:42:32.556 --> 00:42:33.821
So in this study,

00:42:33.821 --> 00:42:38.403
ultimately 225 women
participated in the intervention.

00:42:39.417 --> 00:42:42.570
About two-thirds of them were Venezuelan,

00:42:42.570 --> 00:42:45.120
they were 36 years old on average,

00:42:45.120 --> 00:42:47.040
about half of them were unemployed.

00:42:47.040 --> 00:42:48.570
And among those who were employed,

00:42:48.570 --> 00:42:50.910
most of that was informal labor,

00:42:50.910 --> 00:42:54.289
so really unstable kind
of work situations.

00:42:54.289 --> 00:42:58.620
And a quarter of those had
arrived within the past year and

00:42:58.620 --> 00:42:59.850
then another half I think had

00:42:59.850 --> 00:43:01.893
arrived within the past three years.

00:43:02.991 --> 00:43:06.690
So we looked at their kind
of wellbeing outcomes,

00:43:06.690 --> 00:43:08.070
a set of selected outcomes that

00:43:08.070 --> 00:43:10.050
mapped on to those priority outcomes

00:43:10.050 --> 00:43:13.230
we identified through that
formative research process and

00:43:13.230 --> 00:43:16.290
examined changes from
baseline to end line,

00:43:16.290 --> 00:43:19.830
which was about five weeks
and then 10 weeks later.

00:43:19.830 --> 00:43:23.152
And ultimately what we found
were moderate improvements in

00:43:23.152 --> 00:43:25.494
the positive mental health outcomes.

00:43:25.494 --> 00:43:28.040
We saw improvements in
psychosocial wellbeing,

00:43:28.040 --> 00:43:31.352
community connectedness
and sense of safety.

00:43:31.352 --> 00:43:33.540
But again, we observed this pattern

00:43:33.540 --> 00:43:34.950
where people initially get better

00:43:34.950 --> 00:43:37.620
and then after the intervention
ends they kind of return

00:43:37.620 --> 00:43:38.945
toward where they started,

00:43:38.945 --> 00:43:41.460
which is something we need
to to figure out as a field.

00:43:41.460 --> 00:43:45.390
I think what we didn't see any
significant changes in were

00:43:45.390 --> 00:43:47.520
more of those problem focused
outcomes and these are the

00:43:47.520 --> 00:43:49.950
outcomes that we often measure
in mental health research.

00:43:49.950 --> 00:43:53.472
Things like distress functioning, coping.

00:43:53.472 --> 00:43:56.137
But what I think was really
interesting here was that the

00:43:56.137 --> 00:43:59.760
groups that we saw the greatest
improvement in were those

00:43:59.760 --> 00:44:04.260
women who were employed, women
who started off a bit worse,

00:44:04.260 --> 00:44:06.720
so had higher distress or lower wellbeing,

00:44:06.720 --> 00:44:07.785
which makes sense.

00:44:07.785 --> 00:44:11.125
And also women who attended more sessions.

00:44:11.125 --> 00:44:13.920
The reason I say this is
interesting is I think the

00:44:13.920 --> 00:44:16.650
employment piece comes
out in two ways here.

00:44:16.650 --> 00:44:17.850
First, we saw that women

00:44:17.850 --> 00:44:20.730
who were employed who had more economic

00:44:20.730 --> 00:44:23.370
stability responded better
to the intervention,

00:44:23.370 --> 00:44:25.296
they improved at a greater rate.

00:44:25.296 --> 00:44:28.596
We also saw that jobs were a
barrier to participation in the

00:44:28.596 --> 00:44:31.501
women when they were choosing
between whether to to work

00:44:31.501 --> 00:44:35.010
that day or come to the intervention.

00:44:35.010 --> 00:44:36.390
Of course they would choose work.

00:44:36.390 --> 00:44:38.197
And so this made this complicated
situation where we thought

00:44:38.197 --> 00:44:41.332
the women who could benefit
the most actually had the least

00:44:41.332 --> 00:44:43.837
access or ability to participate.

00:44:43.837 --> 00:44:47.371
And so this has led us to
a project that's just now

00:44:47.371 --> 00:44:51.660
beginning where we are now
still working in Ecuador with

00:44:51.660 --> 00:44:55.307
HIAs to co-design implementation
strategies to think about

00:44:55.307 --> 00:44:59.220
how we integrate an
MHPSS intervention like

00:44:59.220 --> 00:45:02.280
the one I just shared within
their economic services.

00:45:02.280 --> 00:45:07.280
And we're doing that through
integrating their MHPSS

00:45:07.410 --> 00:45:10.382
services with an intervention
known as the socioeconomic

00:45:10.382 --> 00:45:12.930
support program for those who

00:45:12.930 --> 00:45:15.060
might be familiar with
the graduation model.

00:45:15.060 --> 00:45:17.986
It's kind of an abbreviated
version of that that starts by

00:45:17.986 --> 00:45:20.850
identifying and engaging families,

00:45:20.850 --> 00:45:23.311
migrant families who
are in extreme poverty,

00:45:23.311 --> 00:45:26.101
providing some basic
con consumption support,

00:45:26.101 --> 00:45:28.702
so cash or vouchers for
food and things like that.

00:45:28.702 --> 00:45:32.730
And then providing training
and how to save money,

00:45:32.730 --> 00:45:35.908
financial literacy skills for
employment and families enter

00:45:35.908 --> 00:45:39.999
into what's called an employment
or entrepreneurship school

00:45:39.999 --> 00:45:42.960
that provides them with the
support and skills to ultimately

00:45:42.960 --> 00:45:45.720
start a business or get a job.

00:45:45.720 --> 00:45:48.720
And we're currently in this
process of like mapping all of

00:45:48.720 --> 00:45:51.305
the details of this very
complicated intervention and trying

00:45:51.305 --> 00:45:53.670
to think about how and where

00:45:53.670 --> 00:45:57.080
to integrate those MHPSS supports.

00:45:57.080 --> 00:45:59.670
All right, the last
thing I'll just end with

00:45:59.670 --> 00:46:02.755
is one more project that
actually ends on Friday.

00:46:02.755 --> 00:46:07.137
So these analyses are literally
just being done as we speak,

00:46:07.137 --> 00:46:10.770
but this is a project again
with HIAs in Columbia where

00:46:10.770 --> 00:46:15.660
now we're trying to understand
what are the relative effects

00:46:15.660 --> 00:46:17.310
of different integration,

00:46:17.310 --> 00:46:19.978
multi-sectoral integration
approaches as far as

00:46:19.978 --> 00:46:23.043
implementation and effectiveness outcomes.

00:46:23.910 --> 00:46:25.050
So here we're working with

00:46:25.050 --> 00:46:27.660
a scalable intervention developed

00:46:27.660 --> 00:46:29.040
by the World Health Organization

00:46:29.040 --> 00:46:32.280
known as Problem
Management plus or PM plus

00:46:32.280 --> 00:46:34.050
in its group format.

00:46:34.050 --> 00:46:35.100
And this is an intervention

00:46:35.100 --> 00:46:37.200
that's had a large growing evidence base.

00:46:37.200 --> 00:46:40.170
It's based on cognitive behavioral
therapy and a lot of some

00:46:40.170 --> 00:46:42.600
of the common kind of problem
solving skills that are

00:46:42.600 --> 00:46:46.111
central to a lot of mental
health interventions.

00:46:46.111 --> 00:46:48.600
And what we're testing, like I said,

00:46:48.600 --> 00:46:51.742
are two models of how to
integrate this intervention within

00:46:51.742 --> 00:46:56.640
protection services,
community-based protection services.

00:46:56.640 --> 00:46:59.583
We're working in three
communities in the area of in

00:46:59.583 --> 00:47:04.583
Northern Columbia where a
lot of migrants have settled.

00:47:04.869 --> 00:47:08.160
And the first condition we're
comparing is what I'll refer

00:47:08.160 --> 00:47:10.770
to as the traditional task sharing model.

00:47:10.770 --> 00:47:13.890
And so in this model we have
a psychologist or a mental

00:47:13.890 --> 00:47:17.312
health specialist who trains
and supervises a group of,

00:47:17.312 --> 00:47:19.950
we'll say non-specialists,

00:47:19.950 --> 00:47:21.778
people without formal
mental health training

00:47:21.778 --> 00:47:24.738
to deliver this intervention.

00:47:24.738 --> 00:47:26.820
And the people we've decided to train

00:47:26.820 --> 00:47:28.770
are similar to the Nguvu program.

00:47:28.770 --> 00:47:31.290
They're community women from
the community who are working

00:47:31.290 --> 00:47:35.520
with HIAs and serving as
kind of social promoters

00:47:35.520 --> 00:47:37.050
of protection services.

00:47:37.050 --> 00:47:38.790
They kind of serve as a liaison between

00:47:38.790 --> 00:47:40.226
HIAs and the community,

00:47:40.226 --> 00:47:43.680
do community outreach and
provide kind of some basic

00:47:43.680 --> 00:47:45.963
services and supports
at the community level.

00:47:47.155 --> 00:47:48.750
And so in our first phase of this project,

00:47:48.750 --> 00:47:52.470
we trained a group of I think
six to eight social worker

00:47:52.470 --> 00:47:56.160
community promoters to provide
this intervention to groups

00:47:56.160 --> 00:47:57.705
of women in their community.

00:47:57.705 --> 00:48:00.542
And I'll consider this kind
of a partial integration model

00:48:00.542 --> 00:48:04.110
because it still has some
reliance on that mental health

00:48:04.110 --> 00:48:07.620
specialist to provide that
training and supervision.

00:48:07.620 --> 00:48:09.024
So in many ways it's still expensive.

00:48:09.024 --> 00:48:11.704
It still might not be as scalable because

00:48:11.704 --> 00:48:15.004
it still requires the time, energy,

00:48:15.004 --> 00:48:18.052
and salary of a full-time psychologist

00:48:18.052 --> 00:48:20.133
in order to implement this model.

00:48:21.321 --> 00:48:24.750
The second condition for the
study is what we call the

00:48:24.750 --> 00:48:26.280
cascading cash sharing model.

00:48:26.280 --> 00:48:29.340
And so here we're further
decentralizing a lot of the roles

00:48:29.340 --> 00:48:32.752
and activities of the
psychologists to community members.

00:48:32.752 --> 00:48:37.162
And this is what we're also
calling more full integration of

00:48:37.162 --> 00:48:41.065
MHPSS into the community-based
protection system.

00:48:41.065 --> 00:48:44.084
So in this model, what we found is,

00:48:44.084 --> 00:48:48.210
or what we did is we had a
psychologist train a group of the

00:48:48.210 --> 00:48:50.850
women we had trained in the
initial phase to actually be

00:48:50.850 --> 00:48:53.382
trainers and supervisors themselves.

00:48:53.382 --> 00:48:56.692
And then those community
promoters trained a new group of

00:48:56.692 --> 00:49:00.060
women who are community
promoters to provide the

00:49:00.060 --> 00:49:05.060
intervention to other women in
their community and served as

00:49:05.400 --> 00:49:06.750
kind of trainers and supervisors

00:49:06.750 --> 00:49:07.887
throughout that whole process.

00:49:07.887 --> 00:49:10.320
And so while the psychologist
is still a piece of this,

00:49:10.320 --> 00:49:13.997
they're not requiring or not
providing that regular weekly

00:49:13.997 --> 00:49:15.780
supervision and support but

00:49:15.780 --> 00:49:19.599
rather on more of a kind
of consultative basis.

00:49:19.599 --> 00:49:22.290
So what we found, and
again these are very,

00:49:22.290 --> 00:49:24.038
very preliminary results.

00:49:24.038 --> 00:49:28.170
So we did observe just between
people who received the

00:49:28.170 --> 00:49:29.820
intervention and those who did not.

00:49:29.820 --> 00:49:32.640
We did observe an
additional, or excuse me,

00:49:32.640 --> 00:49:36.090
an initial a reduction in
depressive symptoms during the

00:49:36.090 --> 00:49:37.732
intervention period.

00:49:37.732 --> 00:49:40.200
But again, we still see
that kind of regression

00:49:40.200 --> 00:49:41.475
back toward baseline,

00:49:41.475 --> 00:49:43.710
which is something that is important

00:49:43.710 --> 00:49:45.780
for us to to grapple with.

00:49:45.780 --> 00:49:48.210
But what I think is the really
exciting finding here is when

00:49:48.210 --> 00:49:50.910
we compare those two
implementation strategies,

00:49:50.910 --> 00:49:52.497
those two models of integration,

00:49:52.497 --> 00:49:56.880
we actually see very similar
patterns as far as depression

00:49:56.880 --> 00:50:00.540
outcomes where the trajectories
of depression among people

00:50:00.540 --> 00:50:04.595
who received the intervention
from from community promoters

00:50:04.595 --> 00:50:08.310
trained by specialists was
very similar to people who

00:50:08.310 --> 00:50:10.672
received the intervention from
community promoters who were

00:50:10.672 --> 00:50:14.400
trained and supervised by
other community promoters.

00:50:14.400 --> 00:50:18.453
So that fully integrated
community-based protection model.

00:50:19.710 --> 00:50:22.026
Our preliminary results also
suggest that we see comparable

00:50:22.026 --> 00:50:24.755
fidelity and competencies among
our providers between these

00:50:24.755 --> 00:50:28.473
two study conditions, which
I think is quite promising.

00:50:30.390 --> 00:50:31.805
So just as far as conclusions,

00:50:31.805 --> 00:50:34.230
I think there's still a lot to learn,

00:50:34.230 --> 00:50:37.280
but I think what we have slowly
been learning along the way

00:50:37.280 --> 00:50:40.391
is that integrating MHPSS
into other sectors like

00:50:40.391 --> 00:50:44.605
protection like livelihoods
seems to be quite relevant to

00:50:44.605 --> 00:50:47.078
the needs of these communities.

00:50:47.078 --> 00:50:49.740
We also can see that
it's feasible to do that.

00:50:49.740 --> 00:50:51.840
While there are all of these
challenges I shared with

00:50:51.840 --> 00:50:53.760
funding and organizational mandates

00:50:53.760 --> 00:50:55.091
and all of these other factors,

00:50:55.091 --> 00:50:57.261
it does appear that it is
feasible for us to design and

00:50:57.261 --> 00:51:01.532
integrate these services as
long as we meaningfully engage

00:51:01.532 --> 00:51:05.322
and kind of co-design these
approaches with people,

00:51:05.322 --> 00:51:08.115
representatives from other sectors.

00:51:08.115 --> 00:51:11.430
And we have some preliminary
evidence that the quality and

00:51:11.430 --> 00:51:13.990
effectiveness of the these
mental health interventions can

00:51:13.990 --> 00:51:17.346
be maintained when we integrate
them within other sectors

00:51:17.346 --> 00:51:20.700
and that we don't kind of
dilute the mental health effects

00:51:20.700 --> 00:51:22.110
by transitioning them into

00:51:22.110 --> 00:51:24.993
other sectors and spaces and systems.

00:51:25.890 --> 00:51:28.380
There's still a lot we need to
know like how do we actually

00:51:28.380 --> 00:51:30.270
sustain the benefits
of these interventions?

00:51:30.270 --> 00:51:32.940
How do we reconceptualize these
interventions so they might

00:51:32.940 --> 00:51:36.600
not be kind of an insular process,

00:51:36.600 --> 00:51:38.628
but we can provide some
ongoing support to people to

00:51:38.628 --> 00:51:40.392
maintain those benefits.

00:51:40.392 --> 00:51:43.575
How do we think about the
kind of structural and

00:51:43.575 --> 00:51:46.530
interinstitutional determinants
which we haven't really

00:51:46.530 --> 00:51:48.000
captured here?

00:51:48.000 --> 00:51:50.400
There's a lot we still need to
know and a lot of questions,

00:51:50.400 --> 00:51:53.730
which is why I would like to
end by saying thank you so much

00:51:53.730 --> 00:51:56.021
for your time and I'd love
to hear your thoughts and

00:51:56.021 --> 00:51:57.858
questions going forward.

00:51:57.858 --> 00:52:01.305
I will just say also
big thanks to our teams.

00:52:01.305 --> 00:52:03.891
These are some photos from our
different research teams on

00:52:03.891 --> 00:52:05.604
these projects and for me,

00:52:05.604 --> 00:52:09.540
by far the best part of this
work is building relationships

00:52:09.540 --> 00:52:10.830
and the the collaboration.

00:52:10.830 --> 00:52:14.711
So I can't thank these
people enough. Thank you.

00:52:14.711 --> 00:52:16.961
(clapping)

00:52:18.990 --> 00:52:23.803
- We have a question on zoom from Melody.

00:52:24.987 --> 00:52:29.987
So she was asking if you know,

00:52:30.319 --> 00:52:33.792
what is the reason

00:52:33.792 --> 00:52:38.663
or if you can speculate
why in the Nyarugusu study

00:52:39.760 --> 00:52:44.760
you would see such a high staff turnover?

00:52:44.829 --> 00:52:46.590
- That is a good question.

00:52:46.590 --> 00:52:51.300
So I think we saw high staff
turnover at multiple levels.

00:52:51.300 --> 00:52:54.190
I actually, so as far
as the staff, I kind of,

00:52:54.190 --> 00:52:57.569
the people most involved in implementation

00:52:57.569 --> 00:52:59.246
were our facilitators,

00:52:59.246 --> 00:53:01.620
the counselors we trained.

00:53:01.620 --> 00:53:03.750
The main cause of turnover

00:53:03.750 --> 00:53:06.606
there was actually resettlement, which is

00:53:06.606 --> 00:53:07.830
a good reason for turnover to happen.

00:53:07.830 --> 00:53:09.480
There was active resettlement happening

00:53:09.480 --> 00:53:11.730
in the beginning stages of the project.

00:53:11.730 --> 00:53:13.885
That was actually the largest
reason that participants

00:53:13.885 --> 00:53:16.950
dropped out of the program as well.

00:53:16.950 --> 00:53:18.780
But at slightly higher
levels and Kaveh and I

00:53:18.780 --> 00:53:20.040
were talking about this earlier,

00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:23.520
I think it is really hard to
do this work and I think in a

00:53:23.520 --> 00:53:28.403
place like Nyarugusu a really
protracted complex context,

00:53:28.403 --> 00:53:32.130
a lot of the staff kind of the field staff

00:53:32.130 --> 00:53:33.120
are really burnt out.

00:53:33.120 --> 00:53:35.340
And so we saw a lot of turnover,

00:53:35.340 --> 00:53:38.148
people moving between sites.
That was quite common.

00:53:38.148 --> 00:53:41.250
Unfortunately we also saw
that at the higher levels.

00:53:41.250 --> 00:53:44.340
One of our initial partners
on the project had another job

00:53:44.340 --> 00:53:46.925
opportunity and so ultimately
left the organization and that

00:53:46.925 --> 00:53:51.240
I think happened less
frequently, but it was pretty,

00:53:51.240 --> 00:53:54.780
it had a pretty big impact on
our program cause I think she

00:53:54.780 --> 00:53:56.970
was really the champion of
this approach within her

00:53:56.970 --> 00:53:59.649
organization at the headquarters level.

00:53:59.649 --> 00:54:02.190
So I think there are a lot
of reasons for turnover,

00:54:02.190 --> 00:54:04.050
but I think it's really hard work

00:54:04.050 --> 00:54:06.307
and common to see these levels of turnover

00:54:06.307 --> 00:54:08.133
at the field staff level.

00:54:10.830 --> 00:54:12.030
- Can I ask a question?

00:54:12.030 --> 00:54:13.647
- Yes.

00:54:13.647 --> 00:54:15.637
- I just had a question similarly about

00:54:15.637 --> 00:54:18.570
the Nguvu intervention and that in the end

00:54:18.570 --> 00:54:21.120
that you found that it
was better to organize,

00:54:21.120 --> 00:54:25.290
to not organize outcomes or
depressive symptoms or these

00:54:25.290 --> 00:54:28.740
symptoms via you know, PTSD
dep depression anxiety,

00:54:28.740 --> 00:54:31.800
but rather hofu huzuni and the third,

00:54:31.800 --> 00:54:33.225
I just don't wanna butcher that one.

00:54:33.225 --> 00:54:36.282
I'm wondering moving forward, how do you,

00:54:36.282 --> 00:54:40.320
if you wanted to examine any
other kind of conditions in

00:54:40.320 --> 00:54:41.490
relation to mental health,

00:54:41.490 --> 00:54:44.385
like would you use that
as the reference and then

00:54:44.385 --> 00:54:46.380
essentially how do you use that

00:54:46.380 --> 00:54:48.390
moving forward as your measurement?

00:54:48.390 --> 00:54:50.580
- I know because it's probably
going to going to change not

00:54:50.580 --> 00:54:53.820
only across different populations
but also over time too.

00:54:53.820 --> 00:54:56.250
You know, it's a really good question.

00:54:56.250 --> 00:54:57.083
We grapple,

00:54:57.083 --> 00:54:58.140
there's a paper we published on this

00:54:58.140 --> 00:54:59.370
and I'm glad you're asking about this.

00:54:59.370 --> 00:55:01.260
We, surprisingly, it's one of the things

00:55:01.260 --> 00:55:03.090
I'm still fascinated by was that that

00:55:03.090 --> 00:55:06.123
finding or that kind of
triangulation of information.

00:55:07.980 --> 00:55:10.140
One of the ways that we've
thought about this in our group

00:55:10.140 --> 00:55:14.753
is I think there is still
value in using measures across

00:55:14.753 --> 00:55:17.537
multiple contexts for comparison's sake.

00:55:17.537 --> 00:55:19.982
But I think we need to
be more deliberate about

00:55:19.982 --> 00:55:24.982
incorporating items that reflect
local concepts of distress.

00:55:25.332 --> 00:55:29.149
So there's been some work by
Emily Harris and others who

00:55:29.149 --> 00:55:32.511
have like looked mapped all of
like the depression measures

00:55:32.511 --> 00:55:35.871
across the world and how
they've been used and identified

00:55:35.871 --> 00:55:38.690
some unique items across
different contexts.

00:55:38.690 --> 00:55:41.670
And so one of the strategies
they suggest is try to find

00:55:41.670 --> 00:55:45.000
like a core set of items
that you think are helpful to

00:55:45.000 --> 00:55:47.823
measure across multiple
studies and across populations.

00:55:47.823 --> 00:55:50.400
But through this formative
research process,

00:55:50.400 --> 00:55:53.370
identify some items that are
missing that you can then kind

00:55:53.370 --> 00:55:55.490
of integrate into your measures.

00:55:55.490 --> 00:55:57.570
I don't know that I would recommend,

00:55:57.570 --> 00:55:58.500
unless I was working in

00:55:58.500 --> 00:56:00.300
the very same context and could have,

00:56:00.300 --> 00:56:03.660
could validate that these
three concepts of distress

00:56:03.660 --> 00:56:05.667
were still appropriate.

00:56:05.667 --> 00:56:08.130
I wouldn't necessarily
take those three idioms

00:56:08.130 --> 00:56:10.590
and apply them in different places, right?

00:56:10.590 --> 00:56:11.940
Because I don't think they're universal

00:56:11.940 --> 00:56:13.710
or even kind of static.

00:56:13.710 --> 00:56:16.680
I think they, these things are
fluid and and quite dynamic.

00:56:16.680 --> 00:56:19.390
So we have to kind of think
more flexibly about how we

00:56:19.390 --> 00:56:21.720
design our measures to enable

00:56:21.720 --> 00:56:24.330
kind of both of those comparisons.

00:56:24.330 --> 00:56:26.190
Does that answer your question?

00:56:26.190 --> 00:56:27.720
- Yeah, I have a very small follow up.

00:56:27.720 --> 00:56:28.553
I'm sorry.

00:56:28.553 --> 00:56:30.300
This is like very applicable
to my work in that.

00:56:30.300 --> 00:56:32.764
You might find for instance,

00:56:32.764 --> 00:56:36.466
that somebody else has used PCL five or

00:56:36.466 --> 00:56:39.028
the trial questionnaire in that country.

00:56:39.028 --> 00:56:41.370
But essentially what
you're arguing is like,

00:56:41.370 --> 00:56:42.870
yeah I might work at this level,

00:56:42.870 --> 00:56:45.630
but if you really wanna get
a more accurate estimate,

00:56:45.630 --> 00:56:48.810
it's incorporating kind of
that variation that's based off

00:56:48.810 --> 00:56:51.870
cultural or you know, the local context

00:56:51.870 --> 00:56:54.060
interpretation of these conditions.

00:56:54.060 --> 00:56:55.380
- Totally yeah and I think,

00:56:55.380 --> 00:56:57.120
I mean one of the things that's hard too

00:56:57.120 --> 00:56:59.027
is like when we looked at the,

00:56:59.027 --> 00:57:02.070
before we did that exploratory
factor analysis and just

00:57:02.070 --> 00:57:04.500
allowed the items to
correlate as they may,

00:57:04.500 --> 00:57:06.960
if you did look at things
like internal consistency

00:57:06.960 --> 00:57:10.397
and the kind of confirmatory
factor analysis,

00:57:10.397 --> 00:57:13.410
like how well did the data fit depression,

00:57:13.410 --> 00:57:17.760
anxiety and PTSD and there
were no like major red flags,

00:57:17.760 --> 00:57:21.150
but it did seem that after
doing this EFA approach that

00:57:21.150 --> 00:57:24.330
actually the data fit the
concepts we heard about in the

00:57:24.330 --> 00:57:27.510
formative research much better
or more naturally perhaps.

00:57:27.510 --> 00:57:30.180
And so I think even if something has been

00:57:30.180 --> 00:57:31.140
validated elsewhere,

00:57:31.140 --> 00:57:33.630
I think it's always still good
for you to kind of explore

00:57:33.630 --> 00:57:35.490
what is missing and how can we think about

00:57:35.490 --> 00:57:37.083
balancing those two approaches.

00:57:41.460 --> 00:57:43.470
- I have two questions related to

00:57:43.470 --> 00:57:45.540
the communities response to

00:57:45.540 --> 00:57:47.340
these mental health interventions.

00:57:47.340 --> 00:57:48.510
The first one is,

00:57:48.510 --> 00:57:52.208
I guess on how the people perceive

00:57:52.208 --> 00:57:54.909
these interventions to be affected or not.

00:57:54.909 --> 00:57:59.909
And especially coming in
I guess as people from

00:58:00.418 --> 00:58:03.721
the northern or more developed countries,

00:58:03.721 --> 00:58:07.445
are there any instances or
risk of cultural appropriation

00:58:07.445 --> 00:58:10.089
of what mental health issues were

00:58:10.089 --> 00:58:11.940
in the local context?

00:58:11.940 --> 00:58:14.220
Especially in, I guess among some of

00:58:14.220 --> 00:58:16.140
the refugee community units,

00:58:16.140 --> 00:58:19.770
patriarchy might be a lot
more of an issue and certain

00:58:19.770 --> 00:58:23.052
practices they have is not,
is quite foreign to us.

00:58:23.052 --> 00:58:26.250
So I think that's the first
question on how you are able to

00:58:26.250 --> 00:58:27.938
navigate that while trying to measure

00:58:27.938 --> 00:58:31.110
mental health conditions.

00:58:31.110 --> 00:58:33.060
And the second one is on,

00:58:33.060 --> 00:58:35.250
I guess once interventions end,

00:58:35.250 --> 00:58:38.400
what is that transition
process like and how is the

00:58:38.400 --> 00:58:41.158
community able to, I guess,

00:58:41.158 --> 00:58:45.360
move forward with it or
continue to benefit from it

00:58:45.360 --> 00:58:47.347
even if programs end.

00:58:47.347 --> 00:58:51.334
- I love both of these questions,
so taking the first one,

00:58:51.334 --> 00:58:56.334
I mean I think the question
around like how communities

00:58:56.711 --> 00:59:01.672
respond I think is one that
is I've evolved or thought

00:59:01.672 --> 00:59:04.582
differently about over time
and it's really kind of what

00:59:04.582 --> 00:59:07.440
I've used as kind of my guide

00:59:07.440 --> 00:59:10.560
in thinking about future
projects and steps.

00:59:10.560 --> 00:59:14.427
I mean I think it ultimately
comes down to where who's

00:59:14.427 --> 00:59:17.985
making, who has the decision
making power and in what the

00:59:17.985 --> 00:59:22.320
intervention looks like and
how it's tested and evaluated.

00:59:22.320 --> 00:59:23.340
I think one of,

00:59:23.340 --> 00:59:26.460
with the example I gave from
Ecuador and Panama where we

00:59:26.460 --> 00:59:28.666
kind of said like, okay the design,

00:59:28.666 --> 00:59:32.670
the intervention based on what
fits will offer some ideas.

00:59:32.670 --> 00:59:37.020
But the community really did design that.

00:59:37.020 --> 00:59:39.030
I think there are a lot of
people who are very skeptical

00:59:39.030 --> 00:59:42.420
about that approach because
there is this interesting debate

00:59:42.420 --> 00:59:45.730
I think in the field
right now between like the

00:59:48.150 --> 00:59:53.133
holding kind of true or kind
of maintaining the fidelity to

00:59:53.133 --> 00:59:56.870
what we have evidence for and
relaxing that toward more of a

00:59:56.870 --> 00:59:59.460
kind of cultural relativistic approach.

00:59:59.460 --> 01:00:02.310
And I have swung a little
bit more in that direction

01:00:02.310 --> 01:00:07.310
recently and and think that we
have kind of the potential to

01:00:08.010 --> 01:00:11.670
do less harm by transitioning
some of that decision making

01:00:11.670 --> 01:00:13.320
power to the community themselves.

01:00:13.320 --> 01:00:14.253
And I think the,

01:00:15.389 --> 01:00:17.700
like is this that
intervention as effective

01:00:17.700 --> 01:00:19.500
as something else? I'm not sure.

01:00:19.500 --> 01:00:22.290
One of the things that I didn't
actually mention here is I'm

01:00:22.290 --> 01:00:23.961
part of that study design.

01:00:23.961 --> 01:00:27.660
We integrated one external
component and did kind of a

01:00:27.660 --> 01:00:29.872
comparative effectiveness
trial comparing two versions of

01:00:29.872 --> 01:00:31.410
the intervention.

01:00:31.410 --> 01:00:34.744
One that was enhanced with
this WHO intervention and one

01:00:34.744 --> 01:00:38.137
was that was the community
design version alone.

01:00:38.137 --> 01:00:42.000
And we actually found there
was only one indication that

01:00:42.000 --> 01:00:43.860
there was, were any differences in their,

01:00:43.860 --> 01:00:46.735
in how people responded to them
or people's trajectories as

01:00:46.735 --> 01:00:48.630
far as their outcomes.

01:00:48.630 --> 01:00:51.090
So I think this is a big
debate at the moment,

01:00:51.090 --> 01:00:55.947
but I guess I am leaning toward
if you enable and facilitate

01:00:55.947 --> 01:00:58.530
the decision making power to
happen within communities,

01:00:58.530 --> 01:01:01.680
I think, I think you run less of a risk

01:01:01.680 --> 01:01:02.850
of some of the points that

01:01:02.850 --> 01:01:03.930
you brought up.

01:01:03.930 --> 01:01:05.610
But it is, yeah,

01:01:05.610 --> 01:01:07.246
there's a lot of debate on that topic.

01:01:07.246 --> 01:01:09.570
Your question around
sustainability is also

01:01:09.570 --> 01:01:10.800
a really good one.

01:01:10.800 --> 01:01:13.380
So the very, the final
project that I mentioned,

01:01:13.380 --> 01:01:15.990
the one in Columbia where we
kind of trained these different

01:01:15.990 --> 01:01:18.390
groups of providers and
counselors and trainers and

01:01:18.390 --> 01:01:21.600
supervisors, the feedback
we got at the very end.

01:01:21.600 --> 01:01:23.910
So we did, we had some focus groups

01:01:23.910 --> 01:01:25.620
with our counselors and our

01:01:25.620 --> 01:01:27.936
trainers and supervisors to say like,

01:01:27.936 --> 01:01:30.778
to get their feedback but
also to go through this

01:01:30.778 --> 01:01:33.628
sustainability planning
process, excuse me,

01:01:33.628 --> 01:01:36.240
where we actually asked them like,

01:01:36.240 --> 01:01:38.880
what would you need to sustain
this program? Is it money?

01:01:38.880 --> 01:01:40.020
Is it space? What?

01:01:40.020 --> 01:01:42.417
Like what would that look
like to you and how would you

01:01:42.417 --> 01:01:44.153
design this going forward?

01:01:44.153 --> 01:01:46.770
And it was interesting and
we're actually thinking of

01:01:46.770 --> 01:01:49.080
talking with the donor
about this for an next,

01:01:49.080 --> 01:01:52.298
the next stage or the next
version iteration of that project

01:01:52.298 --> 01:01:56.433
where what the community wanted
was some kind of formalized

01:01:56.433 --> 01:01:58.288
process of seeing themselves

01:01:58.288 --> 01:02:00.412
as a community-based organization.

01:02:00.412 --> 01:02:03.163
And so, which I thought
was really interesting

01:02:03.163 --> 01:02:05.452
because in some ways

01:02:05.452 --> 01:02:09.780
providing the skills and the
infrastructure for them to

01:02:09.780 --> 01:02:12.155
organize and be seen as kind
of this entity within the

01:02:12.155 --> 01:02:15.781
community that has this set of
skills and that they can then

01:02:15.781 --> 01:02:19.500
kind of leverage in different
ways with the government or

01:02:19.500 --> 01:02:22.260
with different organizations,
I think is really powerful.

01:02:22.260 --> 01:02:24.381
And so that's actually what
we're thinking at this stage is

01:02:24.381 --> 01:02:27.382
how can we better facilitate
that organization and

01:02:27.382 --> 01:02:31.318
mobilization of these women
into groups that can then kind

01:02:31.318 --> 01:02:34.770
of sustain the project in the longer term.

01:02:34.770 --> 01:02:37.184
But I think there are not
a lot of good examples

01:02:37.184 --> 01:02:40.667
of sustainment of these types
of interventions for the

01:02:40.667 --> 01:02:43.803
reasons you're probably alluding to.

01:02:45.249 --> 01:02:48.720
- There is one additional,

01:02:48.720 --> 01:02:51.003
a couple of additional questions here.

01:02:53.010 --> 01:02:56.373
Abre, do you want to ask the questions?

01:02:59.370 --> 01:03:01.020
Well, otherwise I just read,

01:03:01.020 --> 01:03:05.160
so she says that if you would
share some details of how

01:03:05.160 --> 01:03:09.396
about how GPM plus participants
were selected and how groups

01:03:09.396 --> 01:03:11.487
were formed and it,

01:03:11.487 --> 01:03:14.970
did women know each other and
if they knew or did not each

01:03:14.970 --> 01:03:17.680
other, do you think if it influenced

01:03:18.967 --> 01:03:21.810
the impact on the intervention?

01:03:21.810 --> 01:03:24.360
- Good question. And yes, I
rushed through that example,

01:03:24.360 --> 01:03:26.373
so didn't go through
those details. So the,

01:03:26.373 --> 01:03:30.233
for the last study with
the group PM plus model,

01:03:30.233 --> 01:03:33.930
there were selection criteria
to identify women who were

01:03:33.930 --> 01:03:36.563
experiencing some level
moderate level of distress and

01:03:36.563 --> 01:03:39.465
functional impairment as well.

01:03:39.465 --> 01:03:43.200
The women identified actually
came through two sources.

01:03:43.200 --> 01:03:44.160
One was people,

01:03:44.160 --> 01:03:46.680
women who had sought support
through HIAs and then HIAs

01:03:46.680 --> 01:03:48.900
referred them to the program.

01:03:48.900 --> 01:03:50.639
The other was through the facilitators

01:03:50.639 --> 01:03:52.816
or the counselors themselves.

01:03:52.816 --> 01:03:55.613
So them kind of referring women to HIAs

01:03:55.613 --> 01:03:56.967
so that they could participate.

01:03:56.967 --> 01:04:00.120
And so it was kind of two
pronged as far as through the

01:04:00.120 --> 01:04:03.240
humanitarian organization as
well as through the community.

01:04:03.240 --> 01:04:07.840
The second part of that question
was, oh, about how women,

01:04:07.840 --> 01:04:09.270
the groups were formed.

01:04:09.270 --> 01:04:12.300
So the groups were formed
within those three communities.

01:04:12.300 --> 01:04:14.317
They were kind of far
apart from each other.

01:04:14.317 --> 01:04:19.050
And so it is likely that women
knew each other or some women

01:04:19.050 --> 01:04:21.120
might have known each other beforehand.

01:04:21.120 --> 01:04:23.297
I don't think that was
the case all the time.

01:04:23.297 --> 01:04:25.890
We're still analyzing
our qualitative data,

01:04:25.890 --> 01:04:27.930
which is where we would be
able to answer that more

01:04:27.930 --> 01:04:29.460
directly. So I can't say for sure,

01:04:29.460 --> 01:04:32.899
but what I will say is actually
from the Nguvu intervention

01:04:32.899 --> 01:04:34.542
initially we had assumed that

01:04:34.542 --> 01:04:37.380
it was better to keep groups separate,

01:04:37.380 --> 01:04:40.560
not to have kind of friends or
people who knew each other in

01:04:40.560 --> 01:04:43.598
the same groups, especially
when talking about IPV.

01:04:43.598 --> 01:04:47.130
And what we found, I
guess was the opposite,

01:04:47.130 --> 01:04:49.500
that women actually wanted
to kind of maintain their

01:04:49.500 --> 01:04:51.022
existing networks when

01:04:51.022 --> 01:04:53.454
they participated in the intervention.

01:04:53.454 --> 01:04:56.550
The exception being is if
there were was some sort of

01:04:56.550 --> 01:04:57.630
generational difference.

01:04:57.630 --> 01:05:00.060
So women did not want to be younger,

01:05:00.060 --> 01:05:02.610
women did not wanna be in the
same group as an older woman

01:05:02.610 --> 01:05:04.361
for kind of cultural reasons.

01:05:04.361 --> 01:05:08.670
And so we had to stratify
the groups by age,

01:05:08.670 --> 01:05:11.821
but also it was actually
preferred for women from the same

01:05:11.821 --> 01:05:15.246
women's groups to participate
in groups together.

01:05:15.246 --> 01:05:17.460
That answers the question.

01:05:17.460 --> 01:05:21.870
- Okay. And there is
another question from Lami.

01:05:21.870 --> 01:05:24.919
So first she thank you
for the great work and

01:05:24.919 --> 01:05:28.675
presentation and then she
said if you can get some the

01:05:28.675 --> 01:05:32.752
thought as the best practices
and your recommendation as to

01:05:32.752 --> 01:05:36.047
how to evaluate the added
value of multi sexual

01:05:36.047 --> 01:05:39.887
interventions that integrates
mental health with other

01:05:39.887 --> 01:05:42.654
services included cash transfer

01:05:42.654 --> 01:05:46.140
in human humanitarian settings.

01:05:46.140 --> 01:05:49.470
- Yeah, that's also a
really good question.

01:05:49.470 --> 01:05:53.724
So based with my collaborators at HIAs,

01:05:53.724 --> 01:05:56.100
they actually just formed a working group

01:05:56.100 --> 01:05:57.390
then through the IASC,

01:05:57.390 --> 01:06:00.900
the interagency standing
committee on MHPSS

01:06:00.900 --> 01:06:03.270
MHPSS cash and livelihoods.

01:06:03.270 --> 01:06:06.304
So I think there's a lot of
energy and focus on kind of

01:06:06.304 --> 01:06:07.860
understanding how to integrate.

01:06:07.860 --> 01:06:09.240
But as far as how to evaluate,

01:06:09.240 --> 01:06:13.716
I think the next big step is
thinking about is mapping and

01:06:13.716 --> 01:06:16.020
evaluating outcomes,

01:06:16.020 --> 01:06:18.710
both economic and mental
health outcomes and determining

01:06:18.710 --> 01:06:22.212
whether that integration
actually improves those outcomes

01:06:22.212 --> 01:06:24.443
synergistically more than
we would see if they were

01:06:24.443 --> 01:06:26.414
operating independently.

01:06:26.414 --> 01:06:28.080
It's a big project,

01:06:28.080 --> 01:06:29.879
I think that's hoping where we
hope to go with this project

01:06:29.879 --> 01:06:34.762
in Ecuador, but I think
that's a ways down the line.

01:06:34.762 --> 01:06:38.040
The second part of that question

01:06:38.040 --> 01:06:40.983
in addition to evaluating was subject.

01:06:42.540 --> 01:06:43.560
- Yeah, basically, yeah,

01:06:43.560 --> 01:06:47.373
integrating with the cash
transfers in humanitarian setting.

01:06:49.110 --> 01:06:50.520
- This is Dr. Joma Leense,

01:06:50.520 --> 01:06:52.949
the one who just asked the
question about the integration.

01:06:52.949 --> 01:06:55.530
Dr. Greene, I really
appreciate your thoughts

01:06:55.530 --> 01:06:56.363
and the reason I was

01:06:56.363 --> 01:06:58.080
asking about the
recommendations, 'cause you said

01:06:58.080 --> 01:07:01.560
it can be rather messy to
work with the integration of

01:07:01.560 --> 01:07:03.960
services and sometimes
just getting the buy-in.

01:07:03.960 --> 01:07:05.820
And the reason why I'm asking
that is because we're trying

01:07:05.820 --> 01:07:08.850
to do this work in Lebanon
with Syrian refugees

01:07:08.850 --> 01:07:10.230
and Lebanese host communities.

01:07:10.230 --> 01:07:13.260
And one of the major challenges
is sometimes you have

01:07:13.260 --> 01:07:15.529
a combination of things going on.

01:07:15.529 --> 01:07:17.130
We have some services that are
already integrated and nobody

01:07:17.130 --> 01:07:20.220
is doing any evaluation and
it's kind of some form of

01:07:20.220 --> 01:07:23.190
integration and then some that
are looking at it and kind of

01:07:23.190 --> 01:07:25.281
thinking why they added, you know,

01:07:25.281 --> 01:07:28.375
challenges and barriers and
let alone also the identity of

01:07:28.375 --> 01:07:31.511
the refugees that take the cash
transfers that may not want

01:07:31.511 --> 01:07:35.153
to be known for the mental
health and vice versa.

01:07:35.153 --> 01:07:40.153
So I guess the question was
really multi folded is one,

01:07:40.260 --> 01:07:41.760
how do you overcome those challenges

01:07:41.760 --> 01:07:43.890
to get the buy-in for
those different services,

01:07:43.890 --> 01:07:45.780
to talk to each other and to do that.

01:07:45.780 --> 01:07:47.580
And when it comes to evaluation,

01:07:47.580 --> 01:07:49.878
and maybe this is a conversation
beyond today, but I just,

01:07:49.878 --> 01:07:52.230
yeah, I wanted those thoughts about the

01:07:52.230 --> 01:07:53.280
kind of behind the curtain.

01:07:53.280 --> 01:07:54.630
- Yeah, yeah.

01:07:54.630 --> 01:07:56.910
And I'd love to talk more,
so please, please reach out.

01:07:56.910 --> 01:08:00.090
I think it's, as you're saying this,

01:08:00.090 --> 01:08:01.170
I mean it's funny because I,

01:08:01.170 --> 01:08:03.109
one of the challenges
that we've had in our,

01:08:03.109 --> 01:08:06.105
our early attempts to do this
is to actually get even within

01:08:06.105 --> 01:08:08.310
the same organization,

01:08:08.310 --> 01:08:10.260
the economics team and the MHPSS team

01:08:10.260 --> 01:08:11.940
to speak the same language.

01:08:11.940 --> 01:08:14.804
And it's, I think ultimately
what I've arrived at is,

01:08:14.804 --> 01:08:17.969
I think a big takeaway for
me is ensuring that at every

01:08:17.969 --> 01:08:19.852
stage in these projects,

01:08:19.852 --> 01:08:23.010
making sure there's kind of
equal representation across

01:08:23.010 --> 01:08:26.040
sectors from the design,
through the implementation,

01:08:26.040 --> 01:08:27.060
through the evaluation,

01:08:27.060 --> 01:08:29.760
so that you kind of are
aligned throughout the entire

01:08:29.760 --> 01:08:31.770
process. But as you're
talking about evaluation,

01:08:31.770 --> 01:08:33.740
it reminds me one of the things

01:08:33.740 --> 01:08:35.910
that we've come up against
is even just the way

01:08:35.910 --> 01:08:38.040
we operationalize some
of the key outcomes.

01:08:38.040 --> 01:08:40.845
So for example, the economics team,

01:08:40.845 --> 01:08:42.630
one of their main outcomes of this

01:08:42.630 --> 01:08:44.588
intervention is community integration,

01:08:44.588 --> 01:08:47.880
promoting community integration
through this economics and

01:08:47.880 --> 01:08:49.058
livelihoods intervention.

01:08:49.058 --> 01:08:53.189
And so we've been trying to
figure out between the MHPSS

01:08:53.189 --> 01:08:55.710
team and the economics team,
how do we measure that?

01:08:55.710 --> 01:08:59.190
And what we've discovered is
actually our kind of the root

01:08:59.190 --> 01:09:01.890
of what we think community integration is,

01:09:01.890 --> 01:09:02.981
is totally different.

01:09:02.981 --> 01:09:05.220
And this comes down to a conversation

01:09:05.220 --> 01:09:06.720
we had even about social support,

01:09:06.720 --> 01:09:08.310
where the economics team has really been

01:09:08.310 --> 01:09:10.350
focused around instrumental support.

01:09:10.350 --> 01:09:12.827
How many people do you have in
your network who can provide

01:09:12.827 --> 01:09:16.336
a loan if you need, if you
need cash for something.

01:09:16.336 --> 01:09:19.440
Versus the more traditional
mental health intervention is

01:09:19.440 --> 01:09:22.230
like how many people do you
have to talk to or support you

01:09:22.230 --> 01:09:24.669
when you're having trouble with something.

01:09:24.669 --> 01:09:27.507
And so I think even just
aligning the way what we're

01:09:27.507 --> 01:09:29.160
evaluating as far as our

01:09:29.160 --> 01:09:32.138
outcomes across sectors
can be quite challenging.

01:09:32.138 --> 01:09:34.830
The other thing that we've
kind of come up against that

01:09:34.830 --> 01:09:37.530
might be less complex with
cash than some of the other

01:09:37.530 --> 01:09:41.820
interventions is just the
complexity of mental health

01:09:41.820 --> 01:09:44.450
interventions and the
economics interventions make it

01:09:44.450 --> 01:09:48.570
really, I think, hard for us
to isolate impacts, right?

01:09:48.570 --> 01:09:50.540
And also when we're thinking about,

01:09:50.540 --> 01:09:53.596
to your point around participation,

01:09:53.596 --> 01:09:57.060
all of the people participating
in an economics intervention

01:09:57.060 --> 01:09:59.580
might not be appropriate for
the same type of type of mental

01:09:59.580 --> 01:10:00.413
health intervention.

01:10:00.413 --> 01:10:02.460
And so how do we think about, like,

01:10:02.460 --> 01:10:04.694
what are we really evaluating then?

01:10:04.694 --> 01:10:07.970
So I think it's quite complicated
and something we haven't

01:10:07.970 --> 01:10:09.908
fully navigated ourselves yet,

01:10:09.908 --> 01:10:12.300
but I'd love to to chat more

01:10:12.300 --> 01:10:13.860
as you're grappling with this as well.

01:10:13.860 --> 01:10:15.060
Thanks for the question.

01:10:16.202 --> 01:10:20.479
- There is a question by
Charles, do you want to ask?

01:10:20.479 --> 01:10:21.312
- Yeah, sure.

01:10:21.312 --> 01:10:22.200
Yeah. Thank you.

01:10:22.200 --> 01:10:23.040
Thank you Professor Greene.

01:10:23.040 --> 01:10:25.740
I think this is really super
important work and it's right

01:10:25.740 --> 01:10:28.590
on the cutting edge of mental
health and psycho psychosocial

01:10:28.590 --> 01:10:30.060
support and localization.

01:10:30.060 --> 01:10:32.670
And I'm a sociologist and
I've been nine months into 12

01:10:32.670 --> 01:10:35.652
months of ethnographic work
asking mental health workers

01:10:35.652 --> 01:10:38.610
about how they're localizing
their work and the struggles

01:10:38.610 --> 01:10:41.640
that they face as they're
working with refugees in spaces.

01:10:41.640 --> 01:10:44.400
And I think what you're
saying about like having these

01:10:44.400 --> 01:10:47.097
initial assessments about the
drivers of mental health and

01:10:47.097 --> 01:10:49.523
how they should be culturally
situated is super awesome and

01:10:49.523 --> 01:10:53.727
I think it's becoming
more common, but the,

01:10:53.727 --> 01:10:56.610
the examples that you gave in
Panama and Ecuador where the

01:10:56.610 --> 01:10:58.560
actual interventions are culturally based,

01:10:58.560 --> 01:11:01.140
I think that's still something
that there's a lot of space

01:11:01.140 --> 01:11:02.400
to be, a lot of growth.

01:11:02.400 --> 01:11:05.991
I know like bra IRC and a lot
of other organizations are

01:11:05.991 --> 01:11:07.470
all kind of using the same interventions.

01:11:07.470 --> 01:11:10.229
So when I talk to these kind
of small and medium sized

01:11:10.229 --> 01:11:13.390
organizations and the people
who are working within them,

01:11:13.390 --> 01:11:16.373
they have a lot of struggle
because these workers are really

01:11:16.373 --> 01:11:18.619
dedicated to supporting refugees.

01:11:18.619 --> 01:11:22.075
But then when they're trying
to localize the work, it's,

01:11:22.075 --> 01:11:24.729
they don't know what to do
with their expertise, right?

01:11:24.729 --> 01:11:25.562
So their,

01:11:25.562 --> 01:11:28.114
their mental health and
psychosocial support exports, right?

01:11:28.114 --> 01:11:32.726
But if the intervention should
be locally based and ones

01:11:32.726 --> 01:11:36.200
that are determined by
the target community,

01:11:36.200 --> 01:11:40.029
they don't know they, are they
just creating space, right?

01:11:40.029 --> 01:11:42.600
Or do they actually have an
expertise that they're bringing

01:11:42.600 --> 01:11:44.970
to the table but then they can, you know,

01:11:44.970 --> 01:11:47.070
talk about with the refugee community.

01:11:47.070 --> 01:11:50.110
So I was wondering if you could
talk a little bit about how

01:11:50.110 --> 01:11:52.920
people who are struggling
with the localization of these

01:11:52.920 --> 01:11:56.230
programs who wanna do such good work,

01:11:56.230 --> 01:11:58.320
how do you see the line of like

01:11:58.320 --> 01:12:00.150
where your expertise comes in,

01:12:00.150 --> 01:12:03.390
whether it's just a political
project of creating space for

01:12:03.390 --> 01:12:05.430
people alongside giving
the living support,

01:12:05.430 --> 01:12:06.263
things like that.

01:12:06.263 --> 01:12:08.160
So it seems like a
complicated normative space.

01:12:08.160 --> 01:12:10.512
I was just wondering if
you thought about that and,

01:12:10.512 --> 01:12:13.300
and any advice you could
give these experts.

01:12:13.300 --> 01:12:14.890
- Oh, advice, yeah,

01:12:14.890 --> 01:12:17.407
that's a good question and
thanks for those thoughts.

01:12:17.407 --> 01:12:20.419
I mean I, yeah, I mean
our, so our partners,

01:12:20.419 --> 01:12:23.495
I guess my closest partners
in both Ecuador and Panama are

01:12:23.495 --> 01:12:24.887
themselves psychologists,

01:12:24.887 --> 01:12:27.669
but they come from a world
of community psychology,

01:12:27.669 --> 01:12:30.240
which is a discipline that I'm
not as familiar with here in

01:12:30.240 --> 01:12:33.870
the us but I think is something
that is much more robust

01:12:33.870 --> 01:12:35.700
in Latin America, for example.

01:12:35.700 --> 01:12:38.100
And I think more amenable
to some of these,

01:12:38.100 --> 01:12:40.621
I guess more collaborative
approaches to designing and

01:12:40.621 --> 01:12:43.575
thinking about mental
health interventions.

01:12:43.575 --> 01:12:48.575
I mean I what I guess the
discomfort with deviating from

01:12:50.163 --> 01:12:53.850
kind of what is evidence-based
or what we think is

01:12:53.850 --> 01:12:54.772
evidence-based.

01:12:54.772 --> 01:12:58.165
For me, I think the role
maybe of the specialist is to,

01:12:58.165 --> 01:13:02.334
is first to just of making
sure that the interventions

01:13:02.334 --> 01:13:05.370
themselves are safe and
we're not kind of creating a

01:13:05.370 --> 01:13:08.462
situation that could lead to harm.

01:13:08.462 --> 01:13:11.520
I think that is kind of
paramount and kind of a good

01:13:11.520 --> 01:13:14.130
starting point for their
role and something that these

01:13:14.130 --> 01:13:17.500
people have the expertise
to do and the knowledge,

01:13:17.500 --> 01:13:19.493
I mean I think a lot of it,

01:13:19.493 --> 01:13:21.900
and this is a challenge that
I've been talking a lot with

01:13:21.900 --> 01:13:25.800
HIAs about is like these
formative phases take a year and

01:13:25.800 --> 01:13:27.941
it's a lot of building
relationships and a lot of building

01:13:27.941 --> 01:13:31.797
trust and sometimes to humanitarian
organizations don't have

01:13:31.797 --> 01:13:34.410
that luxury of taking a year
to go through this process.

01:13:34.410 --> 01:13:37.530
But I think the benefit and
where we got was a really nice

01:13:37.530 --> 01:13:40.087
place where it was an exchange
between the community and the

01:13:40.087 --> 01:13:45.087
psychologists and the, our team
that felt really horizontal.

01:13:46.200 --> 01:13:48.514
I think it felt really like
they were collaboratively

01:13:48.514 --> 01:13:51.868
working toward an intervention
together as opposed to taking

01:13:51.868 --> 01:13:55.708
something and tweaking
it to fit here and there,

01:13:55.708 --> 01:13:58.563
which I think is what happens
in a lot of adaptations.

01:13:59.986 --> 01:14:01.410
But I think it takes time.

01:14:01.410 --> 01:14:03.923
And so I don't know what the
solution is in a context where

01:14:03.923 --> 01:14:06.030
you don't have that luxury.

01:14:06.030 --> 01:14:08.820
I think that is, that is tricky.

01:14:08.820 --> 01:14:09.653
And I, yeah,

01:14:09.653 --> 01:14:11.730
I don't know if I have an
answer to toward you on that,

01:14:11.730 --> 01:14:14.400
but I think that there still
is a very valuable role

01:14:14.400 --> 01:14:17.460
of specialists and kind of being
facilitators and guides and

01:14:17.460 --> 01:14:19.308
providing, being part of the,

01:14:19.308 --> 01:14:22.863
having a seat at the table but
not necessarily driving it.

01:14:24.420 --> 01:14:25.294
Hope that answered.

01:14:25.294 --> 01:14:26.631
- Yeah, that's great.

01:14:26.631 --> 01:14:27.630
Thank you.

01:14:27.630 --> 01:14:29.091
- Yeah, happy to talk more too.

01:14:29.091 --> 01:14:30.660
- Professor Claire Greene,

01:14:30.660 --> 01:14:33.715
thank you so much for great
talk and was a great question.

01:14:33.715 --> 01:14:35.965
(clapping)

